CinefexSearchData.register("text/28",{"issue":28,"date":"November 1986","pages":[["1","Cover","number 28 $4.95 Canadian - $7.50 cinefex"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 28 - November 1986. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $15; eight issues - $25; twelve issues - $35. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Riverside, California; color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright ® 1986 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. ... the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Janine Pourroy contributing editors Adam Eisenberg Jody Duncan Shay editorial assistant Ann Dredia The Fly Papers For almost thirty years, The Fly has held a cherished place in the hearts and minds of genre film enthusiasts; so the decision to remake it was not surprising. Only its essential premise, however, would be retained. Under the direction of David Cronenberg, the story became one of a scientist whose genes are scrambled with those of a common housefly producing a mutant form that evolves incessantly into something neither human nor insect. Discussing the film and its manifold complexities are director David Cronenberg, special makeup creator Chris Walas, video effects supervisor Lee Wilson and others. Article by Tim Lucas. Putting Big Trouble into Little China For Big Trouble in Little China- a sprawling fantasy-adventure set in an imaginary world under a Chinatown - director John Carpenter needed a special effects facility that could respond to the demands of a script that called for a wide range of makeup and creature effects as well as precision opticals and animation. Rising to the task was Richard Edlund and his Boss Film Corporation who collectively produced a 2000-year-old evil magician, a flying fleshball covered with eyes, several monsters and spirits of indeterminate origin, plus a vast array of lightning effects and other illusions. Article by Janine Pourroy. Building the Body Electric The Short Circuit script had everything - adventure, humor, warmth and John Badham knew immediately that he wanted to direct it. All it needed for success was a very special lead player - a six-foot-tall robot with an engaging personality who could drive trucks, dance disco and chase butterflies. The task of producing this singular performer fell to robot construction supervisor Eric Allard, futurist designer Syd Mead, puppeteer Tony Urbano and physical effects coordinator Chuck Gaspar - all of whom discuss in detail the creation of a very unique Hollywood star. Article by Jody Duncan Shay. FRONT COVER - The frightening, yet pathetic mutant from The Fly. A Nova robot prepares to fire its laser weapon in Short Circuit - BACK COVER. 4 30 50"],["4","The Fly","\"Help me, help me!\" These ticklish words are instantly recognizable to most followers of fantastic films as the last uttered by Andre Delambre, the dashing but careless hero of Kurt Neumann's 1958 film The Fly. The fact that they were spoken - or plaintively squeaked - from the heart of a spider's web, by a scientific genius on the verge of becoming a hairy hexaped's lunch has made them the stuff of which classic scenes are made. It was Delambre's glory to have invented teleportation, which made travel instantaneous, and his folly to have experimentally teleported himself while sharing his sending booth with a common housefly. The result in the receiving booth was a man with the head and claw of a fly, and a fly with a human head and hand. The former begged his wife to swat him to death in a hydraulic press, while its counterpart died just visibly enough to save the suspected murderess from a trip to the asylum. Though the film has dated into a sort of black comedy, the central idea - the literal 'fly in the ointment' - remains as potent as ever. The proof is in David Cronenberg's $15 million contemporary 'remake' of The Fly, produced by Brooksfilms and released by Twentieth Century Fox. The prime movers behind the Fly remake were screenwriter Charles Edward Pogue and producer Stuart Cornfeld, a former comedic actor who had previously produced David Lynch's masterpiece The Elephant Man and the admittedly disastrous National Lampoon's European Vacation. \"When Chuck Pogue came into my office and said he wanted to remake The Fly,\" Cornfeld explained, \"we screened the original film and decided a straight remake wouldn't be as interesting as a change of the basic premise from a head-switching to a metamorphosis.\" The more the two men volleyed the idea, the more Cornfeld warmed to it as Pogue's pitch about a sensitive scientist finding his outer body corrupting into an insectoid mass touched on the theme of the 'inner man' shared by The Elephant Man and Cornfeld's own favorite, Cocteau's Beauty and the Beast. Their conversations were soon discouraged, however, when administrators at Twentieth Century Fox announced that they had reconsidered their initial decision to proceed with the film. At this stage, Cornfeld brought the project to the attention of Mel Brooks, whose Brooksfilms had financed The Elephant Man, and found the veteran actor-writer-director confident about his ability to secure the necessary financing. Just as suddenly, The Fly was on again. The decision to abandon the Delambre clan for greener family trees was made by Pogue's original script which attended the trial-by-science of a married couple, Geoff and Barbara Powell, respectively a scientist and a cover artist of gothic romance paperbacks. Surprisingly, other than the uninspired character names and a rather too-generous attention to Barb's irrelevant career, Pogue's screenplay closely approximates the twists and turns of the finished film. article by At Stuart Cornfeld's suggestion, the script was presented to Tim Lucas director David Cronenberg - a strangely poetic and welljustified choice. Cronenberg had begun his career in Montreal - the setting of the original film - and, over the course of seven features, had indicated a creative obsession with advances in science and technology and their potential for mutating and perverting the human species. What better director to revitalize the original Canadian monster than Canada's most acclaimed horror filmmaker? \"I had always admired David's work,\" said Cornfeld, \"but found it lacking a certain linear quality, which The Fly had. I thought it would be a good project that would enable him to go from A to Z, rather than from A to 10.\" \"There were some brilliant things in Pogue's script,\" Cronenberg appraised, \"things I could relate to, things that felt like me. But I felt the characters and dialogue were rather 1950s. There was a lot of extraneous detail I wasn't interested in, but there was also a core of wonderful details - basically the rethinking of the transformations, the things that happen to the character, the concept of the chromosomal fusion of man and fly. All the little details that people probably think of as uniquely mine were already there.\" The director fully agreed with Cornfeld and Pogue about the need to perform a near-complete overhaul on the original story. \"The first Fly was relatively good, but not great. It's stiff and melodramatic; it doesn't flow. It's a B-movie despite the fact that it was in widescreen and stereophonic sound. On the other hand, it has undeniable moments of power and emotion. The scenes with the kid being shielded from seeing his mother being dragged off to the madhouse, and the role of the kid in general, made the film emotionally involving in a way that not many other horror films of the period were.\" At the time of his reading, Cronenberg was committed to Total Recall, the long-awaited and much-postponed screen adaptation of Philip K. Dick's We Can Remember It For You Wholesale. The project was to continue his collaboration with executive producer Dino De Laurentiis - with whom he had worked happily on The Dead Zone - but after thirteen rewrites on the script, the two were still unable to see eye-to-eye on Dick's immensely challenging paranoid fantasy. In the meantime, Cornfeld had proceeded with The Fly and signed British director Robert Bierman to helm the project. Cornfeld had seen Bierman's horror short, The Dumb Waiter, which he found terrifying and which he believed reflected the kind of suspense necessary for reviving The Fly. \"He also had a good eye for composition and knew how to tell a story through The Fly photographs copyright © 1986 by Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Attila Dory. Additional photographs courtesy Chris Walas, Lee Wilson, Buzz Neidig and Kirk Cheney, Special thanks to Association of Canadian Film Craftspersons, Canadian Association of Motion Picture and Electronic Recording Artists, Prudence Emery, Terry Erdmann, Bill Krohn, Carol McBride, Bette Einbinder and Paulette Levine."],["5","The Fly","The Fly Papers"],["6","The Fly","6 - CINFFEX 28"],["7","The Fly","pictures instead of through dialogue, which I really like.\" With Brooks' participation, a $9 million budget was locked down and shooting was scheduled to take place in Great Britain, with special makeup effects supplied by Christopher Tucker. Everything was moving along confidently until Bierman, in Los Angeles preparing a rewrite, was informed that his young daughter had been killed in an industrial accident in South Africa. The Fly was off again. Three months of frustration followed for Cornfeld, unable to secure a new director to his liking and equally unable to forget his early conversation with David Cronenberg. Upon hearing that Cronenberg's affiliation with Total Recall had terminated, he reproposed The Fly and arranged a conference between the director, himself and executive producer Mel Brooks. \"I hadn't forgotten The Fly,\" Cronenberg commented, \"but I didn't want to go the route of Total Recall again and deal with a script that had a brilliant core and a flawed surface. So I told Mel and Stuart what I wanted to do in terms of a rewrite. It worked out in this case because they liked my ideas. It became a normal process where I'd rewrite pages, have meetings and listen to critiques, but they basically accepted the direction in which I was taking the material.\" The Fly was on again, this time for good and Cronenberg's alterations involved rearranging certain scenes, contemporizing the dialogue and changing Pogue's happily married Thin Man-esque Powells into two attractive strangers initiating an affair before the audience's eyes. As rescripted, Cronenberg's hero became carsick scientist Seth Brundle, ironically named for racing champion Martin Brundle. A pair of slimy executives in the research company underwriting Powell's experiments were fused into Stathis Borans, editor of Particle magazine - named for Lou Stathis, former editor of Heavy Metal's discontinued 'Dossier' section which enthusiastically praised Cronenberg's work on more than one occasion. Veronica Quaife had no such nominal basis, but the notion of a confidant suddenly revealing herself as professional journalist recording every word was based on a true incident in the director's life. Cronenberg's rewrite was also influenced by a reading of James D. Watson's The Double Helix, a refreshingly funny personal record of the discovery of the DNA molecule, which inspired Cronenberg to depict Brundle not as Pogue's romanticized visionary genius, but as a nerdy misfit propelled to his amazing discovery by the promptings of travel-sickness and virginal reclusiveness. \"That book shows how science really works,\" Cronenberg explained, \"and when I read it, it was really just a confirmation of my own understanding - namely, that science works by accident, by intuition, by human eccentricity, by dreams and aspirations. The face of science is a human face: it didn't exist until people made it. So it was natural for me to humanize the founding of teleportation.\" Cronenberg first approached Rick Baker to handle The Fly's extensive special makeup effects chores, having been more than pleased by Baker's contributions to Videodrome. Baker demurred from the project, however, as filming overlapped with the production of Sondra Locke's Ratboy to which he had already committed. Cronenberg then took a step further back into his career, offering the assignment to Chris Walas who had worked with Dick Smith on Scanners and had since made a name for himself on Raiders of the Lost Ark, Gremlins and Enemy Mine, Walas responded positively to Cronenberg's first draft rewrite of the Pogue script and signed onto the project in mid-September 1985. With filming originally scheduled to begin during the last week of November, there was plenty to do and not much time in which to do it. One of the first steps was for Walas and Cronenberg to go over the script together and determine how the transformation would proceed through specific time periods. In the end, it was decided that at least four stages of makeup would be required - ranging from surface cosmetic colorings to a full-body suit - with the actor ultimately replaced by a series of animatronic puppets having different functions and configurations. In trying to come up with designs for the various phases, Walas soon realized what was needed first was a concept for the creature in its final form. Though Walas tends to handle all design chores for Chris Walas, Inc. - his San Rafael-based organization - The Fly's hurried schedule seemed an ideal excuse to reward his coworkers by pooling their brainpower and soliciting conceptual designs from one and all. \"There was a tremendous amount of stuff generated to try and get designs locked down and maximize our preproduction time,\" Walas said. \"David is the best director I've worked with in terms of being able to present his ideas, and he was really good about saying what he wanted, what was good and what needed more work. The only problem was that he was so busy running between Toronto and L.A. that we couldn't always show him the stuff as quickly as we would have liked.\" The concepts submitted to Walas were remarkably varied. Stephan Dupuis, who first worked with Walas on Scanners and now supervises cosmetic makeup appliances for CWI, suggested a makeup-oriented design along the lines of Ben Nye's original 1958 concept, but with a more hybrid fly-human look to the head and some subtle body metamorphoses. Kelly Lepkowsky, the team's mechanical supervisor, also favored a makeup orientation, turning in a design that fitted a human form with hard, insectlike body plating. Harold Weed submitted ideas for a human with flylike features such as an enlarged back-of-thehead, oversize frontal brain lobes and thick fly hairs coming out from behind each nostril. \"At one time the drawings tended to be very insectlike,\" Walas summarized, \"which was too far in one specific direction. David insisted that the evolution was not human or insect, but that the two entities were headed toward some new reality. A lot of the original designs were based on the uniform evolution of a specific creature, so much of the developments that everyone did were symmetrical, balanced designs. David initially thought that might be the way to go, but then Stuart described the story as the ultimate cancer movie' and that this process was actually a horrible disease. Unaware that he is sharing his transmission booth with a common housefly, scientist Seth Brundle - played by Jeff Goldblum in an Oscar-calibre performance - prepares to teleport himself across his laboratory in David Cronenberg's updated remake of The Fly. / Under Cronenberg's direction, the original plight of a man who acquires the head and claw of a fly was altered to something even more insideous. As revised, the fusion of man and fly transpires at the moleculargenetic level, thus damning the careless scientist to a gradual dehumanization process as the fly's genetic structure begins to manifest itself. / Hiding behind a surgical mask, Cronenberg also appeared before the camera playing a gynecologist who delivers Brundle's maggot baby in a chilling nightmare sequence. CINEFEX 28 • 7"],["8","The Fly","crew developed five different progressively less human as the genetic takeover turns his hapless character into a freakish abomination. On location in Toronto, Walas and Stephan Dupuis prepare actor George Chuvalo for a scene in which Brundle - teleportation - snaps an opponent's wrist during a barroom arm wrestling bout. / The effect was achieved rather simply by placing a fiberglass bone fragment on the actor's wrist and then covering it with latex skin Brundle is alarmed to find a worsening skin condition, razor-resistant hairs growing Chris Walas and his CWI makeup concepts for Jeff Goldblum, each possessing enhanced strength in the initial prescored to split open when the wrist was bent back. / The next moming out of his face, and fingemails that are loose aftermath of his and oozing. 8 • CINEFEX 28 David liked that, so we went out and got some very graphic books on disease. We did some designs along those lines as well, but they went a little too much the other way.\" The ultimate design chosen by Cronenberg - contributed by Jonathan Horton - became a fusion of the insect and disease strains that were becoming apparent in the preliminaries, providing a humanoid form with two arms and legs that evolves sickly, sprouting new limbs that become withered appendages instead of functioning units. In essence, the successful design was the one conjuring an unsuccessful design. The useless asymmetry of the Brundlefly - as the doomed scientist glibly refers to his altered state - suggests that the computer's logical fusion of two distinct lifeforms has resulted in a being which serves no purpose in the order of nature. \"Although man and fly are headed toward a specific genetic end,\" Walas commented, \"it is not a balanced or viable end. It's basically a deformed thing that we're seeing, not the robust organism that would spring from a naturally ordained genetic development.\" \"In some respects, this is a nature film I've made,\" noted Cronenberg. \"We are seeing various larval and pupal stages of Brundely, it'sa a Brundlefly. It's a documentary of the creature - how it comes to exist, how it evolves, how it dies. Some of the stuff people see here as being horrific, well, if they saw a science documentary on real flies they would be just as horrified. I was a junior entomologist and loved reading insect books deep into the night and catching butterflies in Toronto's ravines. The film's attention to detail is me playing the role of a naturalist, and it pleases me to have invented an insect and shown people in detail how it works.\" Mel Brooks allowed Cronenberg to make his nature film in his hometown of Toronto, as wife Anne Bancroft had reported well of the facilities and technicians she had observed while filming Agnes of God at Toronto International Studios, some forty miles north of the city in Kleinburg. His salutary advice to the director: \"Remember, David, this is a horror film - don't hold back!\" The first order of the material, from Cronenberg's retailoring of Pogue's expansive script to basically a three-character play, was that quality of performance would be crucial. Several firstchoice actors, reflecting the tastes of the director and producers, were sent copies of the screenplay. \"The first feedback we got, as you might anticipate,\" Cronenberg commented, \"was that most actors did not want to go around with a lot of rubber on, or cover up their faces, or spend five or six hours every morning putting it all on.\" Not everyone was deterred, however. Jeff Goldblum, whom Cronenberg had met while doing a cameo as the actor's no-nonsense boss in John Landis' Into the Night, accepted the role of Brundle in mid-October. Signed for the other leading roles were Geena Davis and John Getz. No newcomer to genre films, Goldblum had made strong impressions in the 1978 remake of Invasion of the Body Snatchers and in The Adventures of Buckaroo Banzai. His multilayered, emotional portrayal in The Fly is conceivably the most textured and pathetic performance to grace a horror film since Boris Karloff's in The Bride of Frankenstein. \"Jeff is very playful and loves horror movies,\" Cronenberg continued. \"He saw this as a terrific opportunity to have a great time and, particularly liking the romanticism of the script, viewed it as his chance to play a romantic lead. Other actors might've seen it as a chance to play a monster - which they didn't especially want to do - but Jeff saw that the film depended on his ability to make the character attractive. He really liked the script and understood it, and he wasn't afraid of covering up his face because he didn't think of it as covering up his face. That's the whole secret, and that's why he's so good.\" Goldblum's arrival in late October signalled the onset of a flurry of activity for the CWI staff, which by then had only a month in which to generate molds and prepare appliance fittings and full-body suits for the latter-stage evolutions. \"Jeff Goldblum is a special effects makeup man's dream come true,\" said Walas. \"He's not only patient with all the horrifying processes you have to go through to get the molds done, but he was also very gracious about getting the makeup on in the morning, wearing it all day and taking it off at night. If you can get an actor who can deal with that, it's very rare - but, if the actor can deal with that and turn in a great performance that makes it all work on screen... well, it's more than anyone can ever really hope for. Jeff is Number One in my book as far as actors I've had to deal with.\" While Walas and company were hard at work, the question still remained of how best to describe and depict the wonder of teleportation on screen. Cronenberg asked Michael Lennick, a Videodrome effects alumnus, for his thoughts on teleportation and received a preposterously thorough and amusing essay in return. \"I can't say that what Michael gave me was influential, but it did prove that the film could only begin to scratch the surface of the discovery. It would take a miniseries to deal fully with teleportation, so I've given only enough information for the audience to understand the basics. Veronica mentions that all forms of transportation will become obsolete, Brundle says that the concepts of boundaries and frontiers will become obsolete, and I don't go much further than that. But the implications are overwhelming. If teleportation works by dismantling you, literally destroying you, then we are not saying that the same molecules are being put back together. You'd need tubes for the molecules to flow through if this were so. So you have raw stock in each case. Someone is disintegrated to his basic elements and then from a pool - the 'plasma pool' as Brundle calls it - what was disintegrated is reconstructed from new molecules. Well, if you could do that, you could also reproduce people and things. You would have the pattern in the computer's memory and it would need only those raw materials to make an exact copy - true duplicates, not clones.\" In the end, Cronenberg concluded that there was no need to delve into these perplexing enigmas, or others such as the device's likely ability to eliminate diseases or commit 'correctable' murder, or its"],["9","The Fly","CINEFEX 28 • 9"],["10","The Fly","10 • CINEFEX 28"],["11","The Fly","incalculable potential for theft. The actual shape of the pods, however, was never in question. When art director Carol Spier asked Cronenberg for his ideas on the matter, the director escorted her into his backyard garage where he showed her the cylinder of his Ducati motorcycle. This, when inverted, enlarged and painted an off-black 'cockroach' color, is almost exactly what appears on screen - a giant Faberge egg as H.R. Giger might have designed one. The three pods stood six-and-a-half feet tall, weighed seven hundred pounds apiece and were made of fiberboard and prop tubing. The inside top and bottom of the pods were equipped with 5K Skypans, which in addition to illumination produced sufficient heat to melt the record end of a Sony Video-8 camera taping a special effect for on-set playback. Knowing the shape of the pod, however, did not answer the question of how to depict teleportation. An early experiment was conducted in Kleinburg involving an argon laser system that could, using smoke and computer-controlled mirrors, create parabolas, circles and other three-dimensional patterns within the telepods. \"We were right on the brink of using them.\" explained cinematographer Mark Irwin, \"but in the end there were too many complications with reflecting the beam. We had to put the laser below the stage, bring the beam up through a column, bounce it off a mirror, then off another mirror above the pod and down to produce a shaft of light rather than a cone. The cone was coming down and bouncing off refractor mirrors and coming up again, and the beams would spread off these mirrors and scan up and down Jeff's body. The shaft would draw a circle all the way around him, burn up and down and he'd disappear. It was like an on-set optical effect.\" This approach bit the dust, however, when it became apparent that it would entail burdening the clean pod design with accessory dead weight, attaching what looked like oversize concert speakers on top, a large tube down the back of the pod to accommodate the beam and the installation of a water-cooling recirculator below the stage. Mirrors would also have to be placed strategically between the pods to zap the beam from one to the other. Together, Cronenberg and Cornfeld decided they didn't want the available technology to upstage what they felt should be basically a simple, elegant, yet disturbing illusion. Hoyt Yeatman of Dream Quest Images was finally chosen as the on-set supervisor of these effects, which employed the company's unique 'real-time' servo-driven motion control system to repeat camera moves with impressive accuracy, usually to the nearest thousandths of an inch. For these shots, Irwin's Arriflex BL3 was supplemented by a Mitchell camera whose stability and registration matched those of Dream Quest's optical printer. This system enabled the camera to track toward the pod's various occupants and witness their disintegration - preceded by a lightburst of eighteen Sylvania flashbulbs, along with variations of gels and diffusion - during the track-in. Yeatman did this by having Irwin photograph each instance in two separate pieces - 1) the pod, occupied, building to the lightburst and 2) the lightburst fading to reveal smoke dissipating in the now-empty pod. Later the two shots were dissolved together with real-time precision at Dream Quest's Culver City workshop. The company also enhanced the illusion with optically printed, luminous 'dazzle' While the original Fly made the incomprehensible flashing process of teleportation more suspenseful and cinematic with a panel of incomprehensible flashing lights, Cronenberg's Fly makes delightfully innovative use of Brundle's computer screen, which virtually becomes the film's fourth character. Unspecified by Cronenberg's script, the computer's personable and often humorously laconic responses to Brundle's typed-in questions were a fringe benefit for computer and visual effects supervisor Wilson, who had codesigned the video effects on Videodrome, used the Atari 1040-ST's 'Neochrome' and 'DEGAS' paint programs to color and design the rough layout of the computer screen and arrange its representation of the teleportation process. The ironic insights of the computer, such as when it reveals that the product of the teleportation of Brundle and fly \"IS... NOT BRUNDLE\" and its sly divination of \"0.0001% ORGANIC MATTER\" in Veronica's teleported stocking, were all Wilson's. \"There's no way that anyone will see much of this stuff until the film is out on video,\" Wilson noted, \"but it does reflect the pleasure I took in my work. One of my favorite touches is the split-screen that parallels Brundle's body components with similar products built into the telepod. For example, on Brundle's side, you might have his blood temperature and, opposite that, I've listed the temperature of the pod's coolant. It was like: 'Hmm, what can we use to parallel his body fat reading? Oh yeah, the neoprene rubber!' Wilson originally subcontracted Vidmax - a Toronto-based computer graphics company managed by Bob Robbins - to provide all of the computer screen materials for The Fly. Design director Earl Huddleston, technical director Mike Ferraro, and animators Tom Bisogno, Jerry Kopan and Paul Griffin provided some beautiful animated representations of teleportation, a track-in through the DNA helices and illustrated footage underscoring the revelation of Brundle's missing 'fly' link using their trademark Synthavision process or straight artwork digitized by computer to 'resemble' Synthavision. \"David originally planned to have all the computer graphics and text as actual video,\" said Wilson. \"Thus they were composed by Vidmax for the television screen. However, our first tests showed that trying to simulate eight or more columns of text on a video monitor made them too small and unreadable. We then decided to compose them for projection instead. The fly graphics sequences remained as video playback. We continued to use the video versions' of the text until principal photography was completed.\" Wilson then took the text designs to Film Effects - another local firm - where they were reaccomplished under his supervision and cut into the film as inserts. After four weeks of selfimposed isolation and ongoing physical deterioration, the frightened Brundle - convinced that he has contracted some strange form of cancer - calls his girlfriend, Veronica Quaife (Geena Davis), and asks her to visit him. / A latex makeup appliance sits on a Jeff Goldblum life-cast prior to application. / To his embarrassment, Brundle attempts to eat an apple in Veronica's presence - and in so doing, reflexively disgorges a stream of corrosive enzyme. As concocted by Stephan Dupuis, the 'vomit-drop' was a syrupy blend of eggs, honey and milk. CINEFEX 28 • 11"],["12","The Fly","progressive disfigurement, genetic structure has been fused with that of a fly. To visual terms, video effects Canadian computer graphics firm - to generate imagery structure, an examination of revealing a fly as the genetic graphics, digitized artwork and conventional animation Seeking the cause of his Brundle delves into his computer database and discovers that his own depict the discovery in supervisor Lee Wilson engaged Vidmax - a suggestive of a dive into Brundle's molecular his altered DNA and a subsequent pullback contaminant. Computer elements were used to create the sequence. 12 * CINEFEX 28 Ultimately, the attractive, easily readable text on display in the film was the work of Film Effects' graphic artist Alan Peppiatt, animation cameraman Charlie Luce, layout man Neil Cawley and optical cameramen Sam Macrie and Jimmy Anapliotis. Unlike the Vidmax computer animation shots designed to be photographed off video monitors, the Film Effects text shots were in 1.85 masters cut into the actual print, sometimes with isolated closeups or in other cases with the artwork enlarged, as in the extreme closeups of the pod's digital timer. These readouts were photographed on standard Oxberry animation stands, with the progressive typing of the blue and amber characters simulated simply by wiping the letters on. The material turned over to editor Ron Sanders was supplied with a threesecond pause after every line which enabled the editor to regulate the speed of the sequence. \"For something that looks relatively simple - text writing itself on a screen - a lot of work had to be done,\" Wilson said. \"When you're shooting that material and putting through 72 frames between each line, at three frames per second it takes some time. There's a lot going on in that master shot with the various flags and counters and things. At times, a number of passes would have to be done to film separate elements. By the end of photography, there was no way of knowing what David might want for closeups, or how much of the master material from each teleportation scene would be needed; so we got everything. There were five teleportation sequences, then all of the computer's questions - so a master and closeup had to be done for each one of those. The shooting ratio was Give Me Everything.\" Brundle's 'state-of-the-art' computer was simply a video monitor mounted in a console created by the art department - the use of an actual computer having been vetoed by the decision to employ a 24-frame video system. \"On Videodrome, we were using Panavision and had a frame synchronizer,\" Wilson offered, \"and it didn't look bad. The material had a grittier look, which David wanted. But for The Fly, they wanted the video as close to perfection as possible. There's no one in Ontario that does 24-frame transfers of original video material, so we'd have these horrible deadlines where we'd get something from the Film Effects lab and we'd have to send it to California and back in four days My feelings are that the quality difference we got wasn't worth the trouble and that a synchronizer would've given us very similar results - especially considering Mark Irwin's great experience with shooting video off screen.\" Filming began on Sunday, December 1, and continued until the 23rd when production broke for a two-week holiday period. The month was largely devoted to dramatic filming, allowing the CWI crew time to prepare for the heavy effects scenes ahead, but two makeup effects sequences - Veronica's dream about giving birth to Brundle's maggot baby and Brundle's snapping of a man's wrist during a barroom arm wrestling bout - were shot in mid-December. Of the thirty-five CWI team members, only Stephan Dupuis and assistant Margaret Bessara, along with Jim Isaac and Walas himself, were present in Toronto during this period. Thus, while his San Rafael crew was still in preproduction and entering the crucial stages of actual construction - under his brother Mark's supervision - Walas was on the other side of the continent overseeing what were admittedly two of the production's simplest effects. For the arm-breaking scene, Stephan Dupuis - who was charged with the overall effect - first considered building up actor George Chuvalo's forearm with a molded fiberglass shell encasing a spring-loaded prop bone which, under pressure, would buckle and snap through the fiberglass. \"This would have made the process more complicated than it needed to be,\" Dupuis realized. \"It would have been doing too much for an effect that would look better if done more simply.\" The final result was achieved by covering Chuvalo's palm and forearm with a piece of foam latex, prescored on the inside, which covered a fiberglass bone piece that extended from his palm to the point of exit. The bone, painted ivory and mottled with stage blood, was held under the latex by having Chuvalo immobilize his wrist as much as possible. Then, at the moment of breakage, by bending his wrist backwards in a jerk, the unjointed fiberglass rod would split the scored area of the latex. The delicacy of the system and the unavoidability of a slight wrist movement during the wrestling action led to some false starts and numerous retakes. Doctoring the piece was not too time-consuming, as it had not been engineered to provide a blood flow, which was voted physically inappropriate. Blood does gush in a separate shot, however, for which Chris Walas prepared a full-sized forearm of foam latex that replaced the actor's own and was molded with interior tubing for the blood. Also filmed during the initial shooting phase was Veronica's horrific nightmare in which Cronenberg - playing her gynecologist - guides her through the delivery of a large, wriggling maggot baby. In the original Pogue script, this scene was not only presented as a waking fact, but was also the capitalized, underlined, exclamation-pointed last scene. \"I thought it was a nice surreal image,\" said Cronenberg, \"but a cheap shot to come that late in the film. One of the first things I said to Mel was that I wanted that scene out and that if he and Stuart were in love with it, I was the wrong director for The Fly. But as I rewrote the script, the pregnancy became an important issue for Brundle and seemed to acquire a subconscious value.\" \"The maggot baby was an incredibly simple rig,\" reported Jim Isaac, its designer and co-operator. \"It was our first rig for the film and we had to get it done in a couple of daysi The creature was actually more detailed than its fleeting on-screen appearance allows one to appreciate, including operational eyes and mouth that are not seen. It was a cable-controlled system - encased in a translucent skin of S-89 latex - composed of styrene discs of narrowing diameter, arranged in largest-tosmallest order and separated by balls which, when appropriate cables were pulled, provided a compound movement. Both the top and bottom segments, which moved independently of one another, were capable of four-way movement. A thorough slick-"],["13","The Fly","ing with Ultraslime and Karo blood completed the effect. When the Christmas holiday was declared, Chris Walas and company were not to be seen signing cards or stringing popcorn. \"By the time I got back to California, it was madness. There were last-minute alterations on things that were pretty far along on the construction line, so that holiday was sheer work, seven days a week, to pull everything together. I think some of us got Christmas Day off.\" When cast and crew reassembled after the New Year, however, Walas and company were prepared for two-and-a-half months of shooting - mostly with Goldblum wearing various degrees of makeup, depending on the stage of his perverse evolution. The Stage I makeup, as seen in the wrist-break scene, resembles a bad allergic rash and was accomplished by applying dabs of red, burgundy, yellow and blue makeup to discolor Goldblum's face, which was also dotted with tiny fly hairs. The condition worsens by Stage Il when Brundle tries unsuccessfully to shave off the thick, bothersome hairs, and then bites off a fingernail, squirting fly goop onto the bathroom mirror. The Stage Il makeup was supplemented by plastic warts and pimples, with the fly hairs - also shown growing from scratches on his back - made of nylon monofilament fishline, trimmed and tapered at the ends and given the illusion of translucence by tints of black ink. For the fly goop effect, Dupuis made three thin, cuplike urethane fingertips from a cast of Goldblum's hand. These appliances, molded in two layers, included channel tubing that enabled two syringes to pump the liquid - a concoction of glycerine, zinc oxide, methycel thickener and yellow food coloring - through holes built into the fingertips below the false nails. The fingertips themselves were made of dental acrylic, colored with translucent paint and inserted - not glued - into precut grooves. The necessity of hiding the apparatus from the camera restricted the actor's movements, and the timeconsuming nature of applying the tubing made it necessary to film the effect out of sequence. \"It took a full day to film that scene,\" Dupuis recalled. \"Those finger appliances were so fragile that they couldn't be kept on for very long - they would start disappearing after awhile because the glycerine ate up the foam. We shot the scenes out of sequence, so we went from the goo to the earlier part of biting the nail off, for which we cut off the tubing and blended down the edges of the appliances.\" Stage III is first observed when Veronica is summoned by a very frightened Brundle after a four-week separation. For a startling scene in which one of Brundle's ears falls off, Dupuis had made a cast of Goldblum's ears; but after adding translucent plastisol ears to the wraparound butterfly-shaped mask, the added width of the actor's head made them appear comically protuberant. Needing a smaller set of ears in a similar configuration, Dupuis snooped around the CWI workshop for an appropriate donor, finally casting the star-quality ears of Michael Jobe. A trigger system unhooked the false ear on cue. Stage IV featured two phases, the difference between them CINEFEX 28 • 13 FO K102 K103 K104 K105 K101 K100"],["14","The Fly","14 * CINEFEX 28"],["15","The Fly","being the change from Goldblum's own body dressed with appliances to the introduction of full-body suits. In October, Goldblum had had his entire body cast in a very awkward position, with arms and legs bent, his torso leaning forward, as well as having separate casts made of his head, teeth and ears. \"We had the chance to see Jeff only a couple of times, which is much less than desirable,\" Walas lamented. \"But we knew going into this that it was going to be one of 'those' shows, with a lot of hard work to be done very quickly.\" A good deal of hit-and-miss experimentation was required to bring the last nonsuit Stage IV-A to life. This makeup stage was intended to bridge the piecemeal body makeups with the fullbody suit. The first attempt had Goldblum wearing a prosthetic face and a Mike Smithson headpiece that made his head larger and bumpier, with nothing appendaged to his body except a hernia bulge' appliance. \"Jeff has a very smooth, muscular body,\" Dupuis explained, \"and the bulge was applied to a mobile area that caused the edges of the bulge to visibly wrinkle. The edges weren't blending in because we were using a flesh-andlavender coloration that tended to wash out under the lights. David decided that we should give him new arms, so we made arms out of foam pieces and glued these onto a leotard he wore over his own arms. We added the same makeup, basically, with a more bruised color configuration of blues, reds and purples, which looked nice; but the arms made him look more like a freak than anything else. For the third try, we made him a new set of arms and put bulges on his back, distorted his body by gluing other pieces of foam onto his leotard and, on the left side of his pelvic area, the bulge.\" The IV-A head form was replaced with a Stage III appliance which covered the top of Goldblum's head and went around his ears, with Dupuis replacing its less-distorted face with that of the Stage IV makeup and blending the edges down. Jonathan Horton semi-supervised the suit production in San Rafael - 'semi-supervised' because intense activity in the shop resulted in people constantly being pulled away from their assigned tasks and switching responsibilities as the timetable demanded. Harold Weed, Keith Edmire, Bill Stoneham, Mark Williams and Conrad Itchener joined Horton in sculpting the body suits, a collective effort that was not without its aesthetic gaffes. \"A team of sculptors worked on the body suits because there was an incredible amount of pressure to get them together as soon as possible and because they had to be done in numerous pieces to move correctly,\" Walas recalled. \"I think we had a week-and-a-half to sculpt, mold and go through every stage. We were limiting ourselves to half a day to sculpt a forearm or the neckpiece. When we actually put the suit together, we began to notice subtle differences in sculpting style. Some pieces worked together great and others were just different. These pieces had to be redressed and recolored to adjust the differences - most of which had to be done on location because we didn't have a chance to suit Jeff until we got there.\" The full-body suits - fleshy and discolored masses of wheals, For scenes requiring Brundle to maneuver about on the ceiling and walls of his laboratory, a giant rotating set - built on the principle of a hamster's wheel - was designed by construction manager Kirk Cheney and fabricated from scratch in only two weeks. Props and set dressing had to be firmly affixed and the camera attached to a floating mount that enabled it to record the action without betraying the effect. / The chain-driven drum - twenty-four feet in diameter - fit the stage with only inches to spare. / David Cronenberg - outfitted with insect antennae and wings - takes a trial run on the nearly completed set. / Exercising his new-found abilities, Brundle clambers across the ceiling of his laboratory. CINEFEX 28 • 15"],["16","The Fly","A latex facial appliance - featured during Brundle's jaunt on the ceiling - is Margaret Bessera. Once in connective areas around the eyes, mouth and neck had to be carefully blended with paint and makeup. / As a final touch, Bessera affixes a human hair wig - complete with balding patches - over an enlarged, bulging cranial piece concealed underneath. / Descending to the floor, Brundle - rapidly losing his inner humanity as well as his outer - persuades experiment-gone-afowl by videotaping a demonstration of his now-perfected flylike glued into position by Stephan Dupuis and place and attached, Veronica to continue her documentation of his eating technique. 16 - CINEFEX 28 warts and diseased protuberances - were made in separate latex pieces that required four hours of dressing and blending to coalesce on the actor. The foam for the suit pieces was run by Brent Baker, Mike Smithson, Harold Weed and Robert Burman. Two separate suits were fashioned for the Stage IV-B and Stage V makeups, with IV-B's prelude of webbing fingers and clustering feet culminating in the cancerous bulges and distortions of V - a suggestion of the creature's final 'Space Bug' manifestation pushing Brundle's human form from the inside out. The face of Brundle's Stage V makeup was designed by The whole was composed of a pants section, a torso section that wrapped under the crotch, separate hands and feet, with a separate upper torso that blended into the neck of the head appliance. Each joining point - at the wrists, ankles and neck - had to be glued down where it overlapped, with thin strips of latex added to blend the junctures. \"When we started out,\" said Dupuis, \"we glued the bottom pants section and the torso together with Kreyton, because the stress and strain was so great around the crotch and pelvic area, along with the problem of foam shrinkage, that it came undone right away. If we had done it that way, he would've spent more time in the makeup room than on the set!\" The suits were redressed, repainted and recolored by Margaret Bessara after their arrival in Kleinburg. Maintenance on the suit was a constant necessity. Effectively buried in latex, Goldblum tended to sweat a great deal - so, especially after a day of spritzing to make its surface glisten for the cameras, the suit absorbed much moisture. Ultimately, the need for spritzing was somewhat lessened by stippling the surface of the suit with SD-89 - a clear, flexible lacquer that gave it a permanent sheen. Also, every time the suit was removed and wrung out, all of the blended areas were torn apart, making each reapplication of the suit as much a mending as a cosmetic enterprise. The human hair wigs that began to appear in the Stage IV and V makeups - allowing large bald spots to appear - were made by Bob Kelly. Two stages of dentures were provided for the same transformation period. In Stage IV, the teeth become crooked, the gums recede and there is a noticeable cleft in the lower gum area. Brundlefly arrives at his Stage V condition by losing his teeth while typing at his computer. A plan for an intermediary set of dentures, allowing one of the false acrylic teeth to dislodge from its slot in closeup was deleted in preproduction. In the finished film, Goldblum merely spits loose teeth onto his keyboard, already slimed with discharges from his fingertips. For the final stage makeup, Goldblum's eyes were fitted with special distorting contact lenses. 'We were thinking about lenses at the beginning, but Jeff was leery about them,\" Dupuis said. \"Chris sculpted the Stage V appliance and gave it a drooping eye, a bit more closed, with the eyelid puffed up. One thing that really bothered people about the makeup was that the eyes still looked completely human. The eyes are the thing you look at in the face, and they were a dead giveaway. We tested old large lenses at first, but they made Jeff look like a cartoon character - like Mickey Mouse with those big black dots for eyes. There aren't many people making special colored lenses in soft form, but Marc-Ami Boyman, an associate producer on the picture, knew a contact lens specialist - Peter Weickoff, our savior - who could do soft prescription lenses. Because the head was sculpted on a slant, what we needed was a largerthan-human-sized lens for one eye and a normal-sized lens for the other, giving a lopsided, irregular effect. Peter did a real nice job. They're very dark brown and have a black marble look In the script - and in a rough cut previewed in Toronto - there appeared two intense effects scenes sandwiched between the revelation of Veronica's pregnancy and her nightmare about its possible result. The first offered the film's only glimpse of Brundlefly in his Stage IV-B full-body suit makeup - handsomely detailed in a foot-to-facial-closeup shot - conducting a bizarre alchemical telepod experiment in his laboratory during which he teleports his pet baboon and a cat together, using his Telepod 3 prototype as a receiver. The pod door swings his Telepos 3 open, releasing a cloud of vapor through which a hellish, chattering monkey-cat lunges forth in attack and then scurries into a corner among some discarded crates. Coming to his senses and perceiving the horror of what he has done, Brundlefly clubs the pitiful freak to death with a steel pipe. When CWI received the screenplay, they were advised to prepare their monkey-cat rigs with a rhesus monkey in mind, but the completed designs had to be speedily reconfigured when Typhoon - a baboon - was cast. Time was too tight to start over from scratch, so Chris Walas took measurements from the baboon's head and enlarged the models' head, height and arm length with cotton and latex build-up and foam stuffing. Otherwise, the monkey-cat was primarily the responsiblity of Kelly Lepkowsky who provided three different rigs to fulfill the creature's various functional requirements. One was a dummy puppet of armature wire and spring legs which was used for wrestling and was also attached to the end of a broomstick for its lunge from the pod. The second monkey-cat, used in those shots requiring independent body convulsions, had a selfcontained motor and a radio-controlled on-off switch. \"Because its movements were so violent, none of us wanted to try grabbing it,\" Lepkowsky said. This second model was held briefly by Goldblum, then thrown to the floor and pulled among the crates by a wire. Finally, a cable-controlled closeup version was built, featuring two fully-mechanical heads with operational eyes, jaws, legs and fingers. The effectiveness of this model's facial expressions - on which Lepkowsky was assisted by Jim Isaac - may well have been the death knell for the sequence. Though the point of this startling scene was the transformation's gradual clouding and perverting of Brundle's scientific reason, some viewers came away with a different interpretation. Cronenberg himself tended to agree. \"I think it would have been too much, too overwhelming a succession of events if we'd left"],["17","The Fly","CINEFEX 28 • 17"],["18","The Fly","18 • CINEFEX 28"],["19","The Fly","that scene in. I think people love the baboon and don't forgive Brundle for teleporting and killing it. The preview audience read the scene as Brundle being cruel to animals. It's a schizophrenic turning point, but that doesn't redeem him in the eyes of most people. The baboon becomes sort of a surrogate mistress, a confidant. But in terms of the anguish it caused the audience, the scene struck many people as being gratuitous.\" A similar, earlier makeup scene involved the teleportation of the baboon's 'brother' whose genetic structure is randomly reinterpreted by Brundle's computer and reintegrated insideout. The inside-out monkey - a mass of convulsing latex, fur and padding - was given cable-controlled neck and jaw movement by Jim Isaac, who operated the model from beneath the raised laboratory set through openings in the pod floor. Blood was pumped through a pressure pot system actuated by a series of solenoids which combined the blood solution with air to give a pulsing and splattering effect. The monkey-cat scene segued into another deleted sequence as the revolted Brundlefly scales the wall of his lab and scrambles through the skylight. He perches on the rooftop in the moonlight and shouts \"Nol\" as if trying to hold back the new insect sensibilities engulfing him. He feels a sudden visceral pain and slips over the edge of the roof, sliding in adhesive jumps and starts down the side of his six-story warehouse building. Slamming onto a corrugated iron awning, his body convulses and he stares in disgust as the 'hernia bulge' on his side splits open revealing a crablike, undeveloped fly's leg, \"No, I won't,\" Brundlefly insists and, bending forward in a chillingly inhuman demonstration of thoracic flexibility, amputates the twitching appendage with his teeth. He spits it out. It slides off the roof and lands in a mud puddle in the alley where it twitches momentarily and then stops. For this passage, Carol Spier designed an impressive threestory facade of the warehouse wall - erected on stage at a 26degree angle. After Goldblum slipped and grabbed and climbed down the first three stories, the wall was redressed with lamps, drainpipes and occasional atmosphere to suggest the next three. The illusion was further assisted by building an adjoining apartment building into the facade's visible corner, each window of which was carefully dressed to imply a different tenant's personality with venetian blinds, potted plants and other characteristic furnishings. Mark Irwin's camera was mounted on one angle of a triangular wooden block that geometrically matched the slant of the wall - giving it a precise up-and-down illusion -while a crane cabled to the side of the facade moved the camera apace with the actor's movements. The area of the wall which Goldblum was to slide upon was lubricated - as was his suit - with K-Y jelly to minimize the surface's abrasion of the latex. \"The construction crew was absolutely great,\" Walas enthused. \"They understood problems and they tried to fix them. When I told lan Frazier that the suit might have some difficulty with those bricks on the wall, they went in and sanded them three times, and it was pretty darn slick.\" But after ten or eleven When next seen, Brundle's body has metamorphosed to the point where a full-body suit was required to depict its diseased protuberances. / For maximum flexibility, the suit was rendered in multiple latex pieces that were carefully overlapped and blended together. Nearing the end of the fourhour makeup regimen, Stephan Dupuis attaches a shoulder piece to the torso. / In a scene cut from the final film, Brundle attempts to find an answer to his plight by teleporting his pet baboon together with a cat. The result is a hellishly malformed, two-headed creature that is quickly put out of its misery. / Guy Hudson adjusts actuating cables leading to one of three monkey-cats constructed for the CINEFEX 28 • 19"],["20","The Fly","In another deleted scene, the distraught Brundle scales the walls of his laboratory and climbs out onto the roof through a skylight. A sudden tumbling down the side of jumps and starts. To achieve designer Carol Spier oversaw construction of a three-story brick facade that was erected on stage at a 26-degree angle to the floor, thus enabling building was supposed to be facade redressed to represent pain engulfs him and he slips over the edge, the building in adhesive the effect, production Goldblum to slide safely down its face. Since the six stories tall, a cut was made mid-scene and the the bottom three stories. 20 • CINEFEX 28 takes, the sopping suit - which had absorbed incredible amounts of the K-Y jelly and glycerine - was declared all but unsalvageable. The hatching of the fly leg was achieved by having Goldblum stand - for an admirable five-and-a-half hours - on a platform below the awning of a separate upright facade with his head, shoulders and arms rising through a concealed opening and blending with a false foam latex torso. Also below the awning was CWI's Guy Hudson, who pushed a cable-controlled leg through a prescored area of the torso. Walas' special recipe 'Ultraslime' and shreds of latex were twisted under the leg to suggest an especially unpleasant afterbirth of gristle that Goldblum had to sever with his fly dentures. (In one fudged take, the actor actually spat out his teeth with the sticky shreddings.) The leg slid off the roof with the tug of a thin wire, and was twitched on the ground by having its cable controls fed through a hole into a pit below the concrete of the set floor and then mounted to hand controllers. In Cronenberg's revised script, the hatching of the leg is followed by a scene in which a bag lady, rummaging through an alley, stumbles across the misshapen Brundlefly. The crazed being attacks her, dissolves her face with his corrosive vomitdrop enzyme and then sucks the liquefied flesh up through an extended fly tongue. The scene was written out before it was filmed, but not before an actress was hired and flown to San Rafael. \"We went through the process of getting the actress into the shop, running all the master molds and getting silicones off of those, which took about a week-and-a-half - and then, sure enough, it was out,\" Walas chuckled. In the film, Brundle makes a first appearance in his Stage IV makeup on the ceiling of his laboratory, his perverse expression shining down on the visiting Veronica. In a series of swift, jetting movements, he maneuvers from the ceiling to the wall to the floor in a crouched, insectlike posture. As any effects devotee will immediately recognize, this striking sequence was made possible by the construction of a rotating set, but the particulars are unique and noteworthy. Almost every rotating room ever built for a motion picture has employed a central axle principle - a precedent which set a conundrum for construction manager Kirk Cheney. \"The area of the laboratory which was reconstructed for the revolving effect had a big window in the wall that would have otherwise hidden the axle from view, so we couldn't do it that way. Since the set couldn't be turned from behind like a ferris wheel, it made sense to try the same principle as a hamster's wheel.\" The result was a giant, chain-driven drum of culvert steel - twentyeight feet in height and twenty-four feet in diameter, with the kitchen portion of the laboratory reconstructed inside - which could be turned 250 degrees by a surrounding outside system of casters riding the grooves of the culvert. Cheney took considerable pride in coming in under his $65,000 budget. \"I subcontracted the assembly of the culvert to a local construction company. It was difficult for them because of the height of the studio. When fully assembled, there was a foot to spare under the culvert and only six inches above, which is tight. Essentially, the final product was created in a single weekend by that three-man crew, my assistant Jim Biehl and myself.\" Two weeks later, Cheney had the three-ton structure - complete with interior set - up and rotating. \"The chain was bolted to the drum in one spot only, so what it did, in essence, was reel in the plate where the bolt and drum were attached.\" An axle brace system was then affixed to the front of the drum, featuring a seat-for-two to accommodate the cinematographer and focus puller. Lighting problems inherent were considerable as the six inches above the culvert left little room for Mark Irwin to illuminate the set from its most logical source, the skylight. The scene s eerie, expressionistic moonlighting was reflected off of six four-by-ten-foot plexiglass mirrors lining the wall opposite the action, each of which was patterned with twisted strips of tape to cast unmoving shadows. Grocery props and junk food containers littering the kitchen had to be painstakingly lacquered with epoxy to prevent cellophane and aluminum wrappers from rattling even slightly in answer to the turning environment's vibrations. At one point, a two-liter Pepsi bottle atop Brundle's refrigerator was discovered to still contain enough cola to be visible when the room was overturned. A ladder had to be brought in to reach the bottle, unscrew the cap and empty the dregs. Among the first to venture a walk on the 'fly wheel' was Cronenberg himself - who donned a pair of pipe cleaner wings and springy antennae for the occasion. Videotaped by a camera rigged to revolve with the set, the director improvised a comic monologue about a failed relationship with a praying mantis. Chris Walas also took a few strolls around the wheel to get a feeling for what the changes in gravity might cause in Goldblum's foam outfit. The answer was nil. \"I found that walking around was more difficult than crawling around it, which was very easy,\" Walas recalled. \"If you're crawling, though, you've got walls at right angles; so you continually get caught at the bottom of the V where the wall joins the ceiling or floor. If you're standing up, there's a need to continually look outside the set to catch your horizon, because there's that moment of not quite getting balanced as you move from wall to wall. It's like crossing a creek from rock to rock and one of the rocks is a turtle that starts moving away. My concern was that Jeff was wearing delicate foam appliances on his hands and feet and that he had to be given the traction necessary for the work he had to do. Also the foam had to hold up to multiple takes.\" Stephan Dupuis took Walas' report to heart and stippled Goldblum's false hands and feet with heavy coats of 407 latex, giving them the grainy tread of a snow tire. It's a fact of nature that all insects experience processes of transformation, and the same goes for the current generation of makeup effects artists who, by and large, are fed up with them. \"To the future transformation scene directors of the"],["21","The Fly","world: 'Forget it!' Walas exclaimed. \"They've been overdone. There is a transformation scene in The Fly, but we consciously tried to avoid the standard cliche of stretching rubber by trying something new. One of my concerns - which I expressed to David early on - was not wanting Brundle's final transformation to be a Howling-style transformation in which the story stops to admire the effects, because that's not what this film is about. In many respects, The Fly is a special effects movie, but first and foremost it's a story of a person and what happens Stage V of Brundle's transformation, which bridges Goldblum's final scene to a series of volatile CWI animatronics inserts, was accommodated by several puppetized models which performed a variety of functions. The first of these, a 'flex-o-jaw' head, was all but abridged from the film's final cut and can be seen only briefly during the shots in which Brundle opens his mouth to an inhumanly wide degree and disgorges his corrosive vomit-drop on Stathis Borans' fist and ankle. Chris Walas and crew unanimously cited Borans' dissolving fist as their single most difficult effect on the show. \"Originally,\" said Walas, \"it was a hand with fingers that fell off as it began its bubbling, smoking action. That wasn't acceptable because it wasn't controllable enough. Then we went to a more mechanical approach, something along the lines of Stripe's smoking skeleton in Gremlins, which was controlled from beneath with the interior collapsing into itself. But that wasn't what David wanted to see. Then we went to a third approach, which was a modification of the technique I had used for the melting heads in Raiders of the Lost Ark, which was a gelatin paste with some different materials added so the look didn't appear too similar.\" Walas' third attempt involved the preparation of a wax hand - built up over plastic skeleton pieces, veinlike strings and acrylic fingernails - which was photographed at four to six frames per second as heat was applied. It was generally agreed that this was the best solution, though it still lacked something crucial that no one had considered - movement. Borans would be feeling pain; he would be trying to free his wrist from Brundle's grip. The shot was later taken by Cronenberg to Film Opticals - a Toronto-based lab - where a matte was placed around the dissolving fist, which was made to move optically against a black background. The dropped-in background was provided by a still frame of Brundle's lab, taken from the last frames of the take in which Borans was knocked below frame by Brundlefly's pounce from the skylight. The dissolving ankle effect was supervised by Harold Weed. The fully mechanical, pre-separated ankle was part of a prop leg slipped inside John Getz' trouser leg, a hole in which allowed the actor's own leg to exit the trousers and slip through an opening in the floor. Everything from the knee down was false. Beneath the stage, operators controlled the movements of the leg and foot with rods, while other rods pulled down on the sections of 'flesh' supporting the sock. Meanwhile, two pumps forced a flow of bicarbonates, Karo blood and dry ice into the CINEFEX 28 • 21"],["22","The Fly","22 - CINEFEX 28"],["23","The Fly","collapsing area, causing a foaming, gory and smoking reaction to the vomit-drop enzyme. The vomit-drop, as with an earlier shot in which Goldblum himself disgorges a mouthful, was a sweet solution prepared by Stephan Dupuis from eggs, honey and milk. Cut from the film was an extended, rather overindulgent shot of Brundle raising Borans' severed foot to his mouth and sucking up the steaming fluids through a pseudo-phallic, eight-inch fly tongue. The shoe-sucking closeup required a separate setup. \"The tongue was built by Donald Bies out of a clear urethane mostly, and it had a couple of brass components in it,\" explained Kelly Lepkowsky, who supervised the mechanical effects. \"One of the big challenges was keeping it as transparent as possible, which eliminated a lot of the stuff we commonly use like plastic and latex. We used BJV 521 urethane extensively for a lot of the clear stuff; but we had trouble molding it, so we found the easiest way to use it was to pull large blocks of it, then machine it or cut it. The exterior of the tongue was made from another of BJ's urethanes, the 512, which was cast in the mold. It's flexible, but after a point becomes brittle and falls apart. There was a separate mechanism on the inside - made of clear plastic - which operated it, and it was covered in another cast piece made of a clear, flexible plastic. One of our constant problems on the set was keeping the thing together. We would get a few takes, then try to dry it off, glue it back together and keep it going.\" Blair Clark built the puppet's special jaw rig, which opened down and outward to release the tongue. The upper lip was cable-controlled, as were the four-way movement eyes which were solid spheres with thin caps snapped over the tops to simulate corneas. The head was operated from behind by a manipulated rod while Chris Walas - with arms dressed in Goldblum's Stage V suit - lay beneath the camera lens, holding the prop shoe up to the fly tongue. Inside the shoe was a hard foam facsimile of Getz's foot, with the ankle hollowed into the shape of a cup to hold enough methylcellulose, bicarbonate of soda, Karo blood, dry ice and hot water to supply the sudsing, glistening effect. A second full-bust puppet - mechanically identical to the first, but with its 'flex-o' jaw replaced by a release mechanism - was used for the shot in which Veronica triggers Brundle's final transformation by striking out and tearing away his lower jaw. There follows a series of isolated closeups as changes erupt from various parts of Brundle's body. First, his feet are shown shedding their skins, releasing a flow of pink goo, weight shifting from one to the other. The transformation feet - developed by Kelly Lepkowsky, Jim Isaac, Keith Edmire and Guy Hudson - had precut skins made of foam latex and, for those areas needing a different texture, urethane foam. The pink secretions were formulated by CWI fluid supervisor Bob Hall who combined methycel, water thickened with binder and disintegrator, food colorings and acrylic tints. The feet themselves were built around rods bent at a 90-degree angle which, after the bend, went below stage where operators twisted and pushed at handles until the feet began to fragment above. A separate rig was made for a closeup of the fly's hooflike foot hatching fully through the skins, which was operated in much the same way with rods pulling apart to stretch the remaining skins from the foot. The transformation legs, which change shape, were achieved with a waist-down puppet manipulated by five operators. The legs were suspended within a four-legged wooden brace and supported by wooden poles, with the right leg specially hinged with a reversible joint at the knee. With the push of a pole from beneath the stage, the leg's human knee snapped back into a rear-hinged, insect position. The second pole was readjusted to simulate the formation of a fourth joint in the left leg. The transformation arm - which clasps Veronica's wrist as its outer skin unfurls to reveal two three-inch insect claws protruding from its spreading fingers - was built on an aluminum armature and cable-controlled, except for the stretching action of the claws which operated off a master-slave system featuring a small hydraulic cylinder. Brundle's inner formation is finally unveiled as the skin on his head loosens and falls away, revealing the Space Bug's head in a sudden heave of flesh and secretions. The transformation head - again supervised by Lepkowsky - was a full-bust mechanism with aluminum understructure and fiberglass outerstructure, skinned with latex and foam latex pieces and animated by cable controls and a master-slave system linking a couple of oil-filled hydraulic cylinders. \"This was the first time we'd ever used anything other than cable pulls, actually,\" Lepkowsky said. \"We needed to have more pushing than pulling, not only on the transformation head, but on several of the rigs. The head was very challenging because we needed something that compressed to fit inside the basic dimensions of a human head, which we pushed to the extreme. It's slightly distorted, but we were able to fit most of the forms of the Space Bug's head inside a facsimile of Jeffs Stage V makeup.\" The transformation head dropped its skins by performing two simultaneous movements. The neck-to-mid-head portion slid forward hydraulically while the eyes and the top of the head (the cable-pulled hemisphere) executed a different forward movement. As an accompaniment to the dropping latex skins, reset how of e a pair of syringes pumped an iridescent flow of 'fly juice' - microencapsulated liquid crystals in an oil base solution. Perhaps the most noticeable detail, the popping and deflation of Brundle's distorted human eyes, was achieved by filling condoms with thinned K-Y jelly, torn shreds of latex to simulate the broken whites of the eyes, food coloring and thread to give them a veiny appearance. The filled condoms were then stretched to a point of tension and the outsides were painted to simulate Goldblum's contact lenses. The reverse sides were glued to three tabs that went to pieces of monofilament fishline which attached to different areas of the head so that as the mechanism expanded, the tabs would pull and break the The final makeup stage of the transformation - seen when Stathis Borans goes to Brundle's lab to destroy the now-dangerous mutant - was a mass of cancerous bulges designed to suggest a giant insect inside the scientist's dehumanized form. / The film culminates with a series of animatronic effects. The first entailed a cable-controlled head and torso whose mouth opened to an inhumanly wide degree and spewed a stream of corrosive enzyme over Borans' fist and ankle. Wearing a glovelike creature hand, Chris Walas grasps a prosthetic leg extension attached at the knee to actor John Getz and rigged from below to 'dissolve' at the ankle. / For a scene eventually cut from the movie, a variant of the same puppet was equipped with a tubular fly tongue designed to suck up the liquefied remains of Boran's severed foot. CINEFEX 28 • 23"],["24","The Fly","equipped with a breakaway jaw designed to come loose in Veronica's hand when she mutant. Yet another puppet subsequent shot in which form - dubbed the 'Space Bug' by the effects crew. / walking rig was devised to illustrate the reconfiguration of Brundle's human leg into a rear-jointed insect limb. / drags Veronica toward one of A second puppet was strikes out at the crazed was employed for a the head splits open revealing Brundle's final As the transformation progresses, a special Attempting to reverse his wretched condition by merging with a normal human being, Brundle - now fully transformed - the telepods. 24 • CINEFEX 28 The two final stages of Brundle's transformation - the Space Bug and Brundlebooth, respectively Stages VI and VII - are fully puppetized creations that bring to The Fly a singularly touching emotional capacity. In charge of bringing these forms to life was Jon Berg - an animatronics and stop-motion veteran who had worked with Chris Walas on several previous projects. In the case of The Fly, miniatures and stop-motion animation were not considered viable options as the production wanted the footage filmed live, so Berg found his venues restricted to the use of full-size puppetry. A number of ideas were kicked around until the confining nature of the lab set dictated the solution with the greatest expression and maneuverability. After a small working model was built to presuppose any problems the puppets might encounter, a life-size Space Bug was sculpted in wet clay - by multiple sculptors to meet its otherwise impossible deadline. The principal sculptor was Berg, who initiated the wire armature form to give the team a sense of scale on the piece. Chris Walas, Mark Williams, Michael Smithson and Valerie Sofranko then broke the structure down into parts. Once its basic forms were roughed in by Williams and Zandra Platzek, individual sculptors stepped in. Chris Walas did the head, right leg and some body work: Michael Smithson the left leg; and Williams and Platzek concentrated on the arms and body. Finishing touches were added by Chris Walas before the sculpture was turned over to Jonathan Horton who did the breakdown silicone molds. The final Space Bug was comprised of fiberglass sections, latex skins, foam latex pieces and intermittent urethane coatings. \"The considerations were time and keeping everything as light as possible to give the operators ease of movement,\" Walas explained. \"If we had made the entire skin out of foam latex, it would have weighed three times as much and taken three times the effort to move. The technique was borrowed from a method I employed on Dragonslayer, which was to use flexible skins only in areas that moved as opposed to giving everything a general movement. That approach, in fact, is much more in line with insects, which have joint areas with moving skin and membranes, unlike the general overall The Space Bug worked off an operator-guided counterbalance system which executed something of an X-Y patter, with the puppet moving in a direction opposite the movements of operator Berg who was harnessed behind the Bug's off-screen, eight-foot tubular steel support hindquarters. By shifting his weight, Berg caused the puppet to emulate his movements to some degree. Inside the Bug, several mechanical linkages were arranged so that as they changed relationship with the primary lever they also altered the body position of the puppet. All of its secondary movements - arms, head, eyes and hands - were worked by additional operators running cable-controlled mechanisms off to the side. The latex skins were moistened with Ultraslime and the iridescent 'fly juice' solution. \"The Bug had limitations,\" Berg permitted. \"There were things it couldn't do. But it could give certain broad movements and the illusion that it could do more which, for me, is one of the best essences of a good effect - giving the audience the assumption that they could have seen more.\" Berg worked closely with Guy Hudson and Donald Bies on the puppet's mechanical aspects, and was assisted in its construction by Jim Isaac and Kelly Lepkowsky. Chris Walas cited the Space Bug as his favorite Fly effect. \"Even though the principles are very basic - it's a big puppet in the truest sense of the word - the actual intricacy of its design is quite innovative. There are a lot of standard cable-controlled techniques applied to the closeup movements of its face and fingers, but the specifics of Jon's slave system are unique and it has a beauty of simplicity and effectiveness. I wish its scene had been longer so that we could've given it more to do.\" The Bug itself was not designed to give more than the illusion of a step or two. For the view of its distorted hooflike limbs shuffling across the lab floor, Berg developed a puppeteercontrolled rig that looked not unlike a motorcycle with monstrous legs instead of wheels. The operator sat atop a cart suspended on a track above the legs, operating their straggling movements through a series of levers and linkages. Mark Walas assisted in the construction. \"I like to think of these puppets as being condensers for the human element behind them,\" Berg offered. \"No matter if they're pulled by a cable, a string or a stick, it's the essence of people putting their energy and sympathy into the thing, based on a sculptural aesthetic which embodies that potential. Keeping that in mind while you're building something can produce an effect on the audience that's more than just a bunch of mechanics moving rubber around. My perception of a good effect is that it's a strange distillation of technological skill and emotional aesthetics that blend together where it shows, but also somewhere inside the people who are working it. If you need six people to operate a puppet that's supposed to be a personality, it's like a little baroque orchestra coming together and having a good enough instrument that all their energies can harmonize on it.\" CWI's final assignment was the Brundlebooth - a literal meshing of the Space Bug and a telepod that results from Brundle's aborted last-ditch attempt to repurify himself by merging genetically with Veronica. In the closing moments of the film, the hideously malformed creature sluices out of a receiving pod, its insect body fused with assorted mechanical material from the sending pod - most notably, three ribbed panels issuing from its back like wings. Chris Walas designed a maquette for the creature, from which the construction of a large version was supervised by Howie Weed and Jonathan Horton using newly minted miscellany and pieces left over from the Space Bug. \"We took the head, arms and stomach section and put them all together to resemble something basically like the Space Bug,\" said Weed, \"then fabricated a completely new back section which was made out of everything but the kitchen sink. We had latex, garbage bags, rubber tubing, bits and pieces"],["25","The Fly","CINEFEX 28 • 25"],["26","The Fly","26 • CINEFEX 28"],["27","The Fly","of stuff that the production sent us from the set; so we had some continuity going on. I think almost everyone in the shop had something to do with it, but the bits and pieces fell down to Jonathan and me. The whole thing was thrown together in about two weeks, from Chris handing us the maquette to us packing it into the box.\" The Brundlebooth was given the illusion of crawling across the floor on its front legs by a crew of nine puppeteers secreted below the raised laboratory set. The creature's head, hands, elbows and trunk were attached to rods lowered through groove tracks cut into the floor. The tracks were lubricated with cornstarch and hidden by a carpet of smoke issuing from the opened pod. On called cues, the puppeteers handling the left and right arm movements alternated in a forward progression while others handled the cable-controlled facial performance. Perhaps the most astonishing aspect of the Space Bug and Brundlebooth puppets is their mechanical ability to perpetuate the moving performance begun by Jeff Goldblum. The entire CWI crew saluted Goldblum's acting and claimed that without his sensitivity to serve as an illustration the puppets might not have had such dramatic capability built into them. It's a testament to the mechanics behind the puppets' eyelids that their opaque insect eyes were capable of registering any emotion at all, but on screen they convey a heart-wrenching, unfocused, almost infantile vulnerability. Both puppets used identical eyes, made of styrene hemispheres with several layers of paint, gloss coatings and metallic dusting powders. As it stands, The Fly concludes with Veronica falling grievously to her knees over the shattered remains of the Brundlebooth. As with Videodrome- the script of which ended with a perverse 'afterlife' orgy which was never filmed - and The Dead Zone for which a final scene between Herbert Lom and Brooke Adams was filmed but deleted, The Fly has also been relieved of a coda sequence. In a surreal dream of Veronica's, the camera moves slowly along a bare branch to its tip where it connects to a translucent amber chrysalis. The chrysalis begins to sway with inner movement and hatches, revealing a human infant wrapped in a pair of graceful, pale blue butterfly wings. The baby unfolds its wings, opens its eyes and flies out of frame. It then lights on the branch, stretches its wings to full size and flies off toward a distant light source. Under Jon Berg's supervision, the dream was filmed in two stop-motion shots (covering 1270 frames) during postproduction at San Francisco's Colossal Pictures. Director of photography for the sequence was Michael Owens and the camera was motion controlled by computer operator John Gazdik. The insert was filmed over a labor-intensive, seventeen-day period, its slightly more generous production schedule having been abbreviated when the filming in Toronto unexpectedly ran a week over schedule. \"I think one of the hardest things anybody can ask an animator to do is to emulate a human, much less a human baby,\" Berg reflected, \" so the project presented a number of technical difficulties. The sculpture, painting and animaRealizing its attempted fusion with Veronica is about to be thwarted, the Space Bug smashes through the door of its telepod. / The fully-mechanical creature as fashioned by a CWI task force under the supervision of Jon Berg. / The walking Space Bug functioned off a counterbalanced slave system, with the puppet moving in a direction opposite to the movements of operator Berg who was harnessed behind the creature's mobile support system. / A more precisely actuated, hands-on variation of the Bug was employed for closeups. / At the instant of teleportation, the Space Bug - overstepping the threshold of its telepod at the time - is fused bodily with the pod itself, producing a scrambled mass of insect and mechanical matter. CINEFEX 28 • 27"],["28","The Fly","manifestation - dubbed the 'Brundlebooth' - was a rod beneath the stage by a crew of nine. Slots in the floor to permit the various rods were concealed by a blanket of smoke and vapor streaming out of the telepod. / In a coda ultimately deleted from dream in which a winged human baby hatches from a chrysalis and takes flight. Rendered in two lengthy figure and was animated - primarily by Jon Berg - The creature's final puppet operated from the film, Veronica - fearfully pregnant with Brundle's child - has a stop-motion shots, the sequence employed an eleven-inch-tall puppet during postproduction. 28 - CINEFEX 28 tion of the figure had to be effective and not laughable, which was a tricky challenge considering the time frame we had.\" Fortunately, Berg's efforts were reinforced by several highly competent team members - Anthony Laudati acted as animation assistant; Blair Clark built the model's armature from a Tom St. Amand design; Donald Bies built the rigging; Kelly Lepkowsky and Jerrold Neidig worked together to fabricate the butterfly wings; and Harold Weed assisted the sculpting, molding and casting of the model when Chris Walas was downed by a three-week bout with pneumonia. When completed, the puppet stood eleven inches high with a twenty-three-inch The sparse scenery of the dream was additionally demanding. \"For the tree,\" Berg explained, \"we made a welded steel frame so that it would be rigid while still offering access inside for rigging. Jonathan Horton and Howie Weed covered it with builtup polyfoam, latex and towel to give it texture, and then painted that up. This served a dual purpose. We didn't have to mold or cast anything, and the rubber allowed the tree to be resilient. When I was working around it, I could touch it without its getting impacted. After the baby was sculpted out of latex and polyfoam, I made some mockup wings and wrapped them around the baby, in a fetal position, and then sculpted the chrysalis over a layer of plaster bandage. Howie finished and molded it, with Chris and me supervising, and we ran vacuformed acetate pieces for the chrysalis which was painted amber.\" Asked for a surreal landscape as a backdrop, Walas designed a densely powerful rear view of basic blacks that was as evocative of a womb as of a never-never land. Walas and Berg had doubts that the dream sequence was necessary as a postscript to Brundle's death, believing that audiences would be too affected by the powerful climax to concentrate on the lighter, ethereal imagery following it. \"I thought the dream was a pretty concept,\" said Berg, \"and a very positive and uplifting idea; but until it was actually screened altogether, there was no way of knowing how it was going to dovetail with the rest of the picture. I had a good sense that it wasn't going to be used, even before we started to work on it, but that wasn't a concern really. We were concerned with giving the very best we were capable of, which I believe we did.\" One would imagine that the jump from a live-action storyline to a stop-motion finale might appear texturally inconsistent at least, but this was not apparently a problem. \"Because it was so stylized,\" Walas explained, 'I don't think audiences would've had a problem with it. It worked as a sequence, and Jon did some really marvelous work on it. The only time I think I saw any strobing at all was on some of the fingertips - which was incredibly tiny work - but it didn't have that stop-motion 'look.\" \"I was probably the dream scene's biggest fan,\" said Stuart Cornfeld, who previewed the film to a Los Angeles audience with the dream intact. \"It had moments in it that were almost unbelievably beautiful, but the audience members we showed it to were still stunned by the climax in the lab and weren't able to settle down enough to really appreciate it.\" Additionally, it was felt that the scene complicated matters by raising more questions than it resolved. In the end, it was cut prior to general release. The Fly is perhaps unimpeachable proof that the art of special makeup effects - long accused of merely sensationalizing an already sensationalized medium - can be used to touch new emotional and intellectual areas of human dreams and human drama. \"It was a very satisfying experience for us,\" Chris Walas reflected. \"In many ways, working a tighter schedule is most satisfying because ingenuity comes much more into play. When something works, you feel like you've really done something; and when it doesn't work, well, the schedule leaves you with a great excuse. Jon Berg and I were recently talking about how much more we would've liked to have given to the picture - because I think The Fly deserved everything we could possibly give it - but there was never enough time. The audience will never know, because it only knows what it's given, but we're still very happy with the way it worked out.\" As usual, the value of David Cronenberg's latest film lies in the questions it raises and in the little things it doesn't say. It demands to be not only watched, but observed. The Fly is not a commentary on the godlessness of the New Biology, nor is it the sort of film one can or should bring such ideas to. If the movie leaves its audience with the impression that even in 1986 there are still things man was meant to leave alone, they are missing the point. \"It's in man's nature to tamper with things,\" Cronenberg emphasized, \"That's what we do, that's what we do best, and that is our nature. For us to build a teleportation device is natural as far as I'm concerned. In Pogue's script, as in the original story, the Fly realizes he's gone mad, gone too far and gone where no man should go, and he feels his machine should be destroyed. Even in the Fifties, that was a Hollywood view of science. But no scientist has ever turned away from an incredible discovery because it had potential dangers. Never. Whether it's good or bad at the moment is not the question. Every scientific advance has killed people or had the potential to kill people, whether it's the invention of the steam engine or the discovery of coal as a power source or the development of the space shuttle. But it's always been Hollywood's romantic, misguided idea that a scientist will destroy his life's work just because he happened to make one stupid mistake. Brundle's plight wasn't the result of a failure in design. It was a failure to notice.\" In this case - to err is human, to remake divine. Tim Lucas is a Cincinnati-based journalist who has been writing for film publications since 1971. Currently he is general editor and coauthor of Video Times' twelve-volume series, Your Movie Guide to Video Tapes and Discs. He also has a regular column in Overview, a magazine on videotape."],["29","The Fly","CINEFEX 28 • 29"],["30","Big Trouble in Little China","PUTTING BIG TROUBLE INTO LITTLE CHINA"],["31","Big Trouble in Little China","\"Making special effects films is not my favorite thing to do,\" admitted director John Carpenter, \"but I've ended up being involved with so many because the kinds of stories I like are fantasies or science fiction or horror - which require some sort of otherworldly, supernatural visions to appear on the screen, and thus require effects of some kind.\" In the case of Big Trouble in Little China, Carpenter found elements from each of his favorite genres rolled into one big, thrill-a-minute package - with some kung fu, comedy and romance thrown in for good measure. \"I say effects films are not my favorite, simply because they're so difficult to do. You don't dial a telephone and say, Listen, I want a shot of a spaceship coming over a mountain.' It just doesn't work that way. But Big Trouble was clearly a unique project - like nothing I'd ever seen before. And truthfully, the issue was that I had always wanted to do a kung fu movie. Always. So there I went.\" The eclectic yarn of a trucker who drives his pig-filled Peterbilt into the midnight fog of Chinatown - and into an exotic world of skullduggery and mysticism - called for the decidedly dazzling application of special effects. \"When I read a screenplay,\" explained Carpenter, \"at the very beginning, I visualize the movie. That's the way I decide whether I'm going to do a film or not. If I'm reading it and I can see the movie in my mind - and I forget that I'm reading - then I'll say: 'Sure. Let's do it.' And as I'm reading the script, I can see the special effects. So what I did on Big Trouble was sit down and write on a separate little script all the effects and what they would look like. That way, I basically had a launching off point. In the beginning, we considered several people to do the effects, and we left it sort of wide open. I had really enjoyed the people at Industrial Light and Magic, who had done Starman. They're awfully nice folks. But while I was happy enough with some of the work they did, some of it I wasn't as happy with. Besides, with ILM located near San Francisco, things become difficult and more expensive having to trek up there from Los Angeles. Twentieth Century Fox had a very good relationship with Richard Edlund and Boss Film - and I've always been impressed by their work - so we decided to go with them. When Richard - who is an extremely professional, extremely talented man - and his people came in, their ideas and input melded together with mine and we were in business.\" For Richard Edlund, the prospect of becoming involved with Big Trouble was equally appealing. \"It was exciting for us because the picture dealt with previously untouched areas of effects work. Taking place as it does in Chinatown and the mythical tunnels underneath Chinatown, it dealt with legendary stories everybody has heard and maybe wondered about - and Big Trouble in Little China photographs copyright © 1986 by Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by John Shannon. Effects unit still photography by Virgil Mirano. having taken tours through Chinatown in San Francisco myself, I was very interested. I was also excited about having the opportunity to work with John Carpenter. He knows what it takes to make a movie work and has dealt with special effects before. He is especially familiar with prosthetic work and monsters, having done some great ones in The Thing. He knows exactly how they should be used on film, too, so there's no confusion as far as he's concerned. And while he had a lot of definite ideas, he was still very open to our input. We would talk about certain things he'd want to see and then we'd come up with sketches - our art director, George Jenson, is an excellent conceptual person. So the idea flow was very exciting.\" The project's intense level of energy was apparent from the beginning, virtually emanating from the entertaining and engaging screenplay. Originally devised as a turn-of-the-century kung fu/western hybrid by writers Gary Goldman and David Z. Weinstein, Big Trouble in Little China was reworked into a contemporary setting by W.D. Richter on the reasonable notion that disbelief is best suspended when not taxed by the overuse of exotic premises. The producers felt that with the distraction of establishing and maintaining a period piece eliminated from the storyline, the magical microcosm of San Francisco's Chinatown could more satisfactorily be explored. Enter trucker Jack Burton - played by Carpenter perennial Kurt Russell - and his Pork Chop Express. After making a delivery in the aftermidnight marketplace, Jack and his friend Wang Chi find themselves immersed in intrigue as they endeavor to rescue a couple of beautiful girls from some very bad guys - doing battle with supernatural foes along the way and ultimately facing down the ultimate evil, Lo Pan. The adventure kicks off when Jack and Wang Chi - in hot pursuit of some gang members who have abducted Wang's fiancee - round a foggy corner and happen upon a funeral procession. From the relative safety of the Peterbilt, Jack and Wang watch, horrified, as the mourners are interrupted by a few rather nasty characters who incite some violent gang warfare. Into the midst of the melee, a vaporous green glow erupts bearing three dangerous and mystical warriors - the Masters of Death - and ultimately Lo Pan himself, who takes charge of the situation causing the surviving gang members to scatter. The green glow effect used in this sequence - and throughout the film as well - was intended as a precursor to Lo Pan's evil presence, whether represented by the master himself or his servants. The choice of color was deliberate and straightforward, based on green being a favorite in oriental culture, as well as on Lo Pan's maniacal obsession with finding a green-eyed girl. The method for realizing the effect was less obvious. \"A lot of effects are arrived at through trial and error,\" commented art director Jenson, \"by trying to find new ways to do things. John Carpenter initially described the green glow as an Alka-Seltzer tablet in water - expanding, yet organic. So I said to Garry Waller, 'What can we do to get that effect?' The notion of that Janine Pourroy organic, effervescent green glow started him thinking, And"],["32","Big Trouble in Little China","setup in preparation for an Trouble in Little China. With a 2000-year-old wizard, an lightning and optical effects, the project would prove to be a special effects field day Corporation. / Boss Film's bossman Richard Edlund surveys with Carpenter a breakaway ceiling that is to heralding the arrival of an unearthly trio of oriental Director John Carpenter checks out a two-camera explosive scene in Big assortment of creepy monsters and a rash of for the artisans and technicians of Boss Film be shattered with pyrotechnic effects spirits - Lightning, Thunder and Rain. 32 → CINEFEX 28 that's the key thing: to give people enough time and leeway to get their juices going so that they can then do something creative. The glow could very well have been an Alka-Seltzer tablet, or an explosion shot at high-speed, or ultraviolet dye injected in the cloud tank. As it turned out, Garry and cameraman Mat Beck came up with something altogether different. In any case, however, we usually go through a variety of earlystage approaches before we nail an effect down into something After doing extensive cloud tank tests wherein ultraviolet dyes were injected into the water and illuminated with black light, the approach was scrapped in favor of a chemicallycreated illusion. By combining certain relative valences of green paint and metallic powder in an acetone base, then heating the ‚\"We concoction to a boil, a roiling lava-like mass resulted. simply heated the mixture on a little chemistry hotplate,\" explained animation supervisor Garry Waller. \"Mat Beck got the camera's fisheye lens down into the pyrex jar and then photographed it. He could adjust the temperature so that the liquid was just below the boiling point. Then by raising the temperature slightly, the mixture would erupt into this gaseous thing. Mat then rear-projected the footage onto a dome, and photographed the dome - which gave the glow a nice soft-edged quality. He developed motion control moves through our electronics where he could very subtly push in and do a zoom on the glow, causing it to come forward. He also used a moire in front of the lens to feather the edge because the edge quality was the most critical part. Originally, the glow was just this round thing that looked neat, but it didn't have any vaporesque edge quality. The moire really made a difference.\" The moire effect was achieved by mounting two separate pieces of glass on turntable-like rigs, spinning them on different axes, and then photographing - frame-by-frame - footage of the acetone and paint mixture through them. Because the moire was basically transparent, but spattered with randomly-placed black spots, the separate spinning of each allowed clear areas of glass to be occasionally eclipsed by black areas. The resulting footage revealed only sections of the green substance at a time; but when run at normal speed, the green glow flickered and warbled as if being looked at it through heat. Lo Pan appears in the alleyway scene as a Mandarin warrior: and although he seems perfectly mortal, when Jack runs over him with his enormous rig, Lo Pan stands unscathed. Laughing demonically at the befuddled Jack, Lo Pan dismisses the trucker with a glare and a smile of, literally, blinding radiance. \"The idea behind Lo Pan's illuminated eyes and smile,\" explained Waller, \"was to create the same look as headlights in the fog. We used a basic slitscan technique that was originally developed by Richard Edlund and Bob Abel for use in commercials - a 'candy apple' sort of look - which we have been experimenting with and developing for future projects. It's based on streak photography -just drawing a dot from the eye, zooming the camera down and panning the dot so it streaks from the time exposure. With Bill Tondreau's software, I could streak a dot of light across the film frame from point to point - which took about forty seconds per frame to expose. I was also able to lower the match-move of Lo Pan's eyes if his eyes blinked or his head moved about, track that and project a beam out of his eyes in perspective, moving it in conjunction with wherever he was looking. That way, the other end of the beam would follow realistically, which is a real breakthrough in this new software. We would then run a moire pattern over the source light which would break it up - interval frames would sometimes reveal a clear hole and sometimes half of a hole - and shoot it frameby-frame. When run at normal speed, the light would scintillate from the source, creating shards.\" Lo Pan, it seems, is a 2000-year-old magician entrapped by an ancient curse in either the withered, wheelchair-ridden body of an old man or the spirit form of a seven-foot-tall warrior. Only by marrying a girl with green eyes - Wang Chi's kidnapped beloved, Miao Yin- and then having her killed can Lo Pan escape the curse of the Ching-ti and possess power and youth eternal. The character's earthbound manifestation as David Lo Pan was achieved via an extensive appliance makeup on actor James Hong, designed and executed by Boss makeup effects expert Steve Johnson. For Johnson, a veteran of the manifold complexities of creature effects for such films as An American Werewolf in London, Ghostbusters and Poltergeist II, Big Trouble's old-age makeup represented a challenging departure. \"Doing the Lo Pan character was probably the thing I was most happy with on the whole show, because I finally got to do an actual 'makeup' for a movie. When I first came out to Hollywood - after I'd grown up in Texas, studying and trying to figure out specialized makeups - I thought all this mechanical stuff was beyond me. I had no idea that I'd end up making a career out of it. I figured I'd just do old-age makeup, maybe an occasional werewolf or zombie for the rest of my life. As it turns out, I was in the business for eight years before I even got the opportunity to do an old-age makeup.\" The Lo Pan old-age makeup utilized ten overlapping facial appliances - as opposed to a one- or two-piece mask - a beard, and a foam rubber bald cap complete with cranial structure, veins and long, sparse hair. Jack Bricker created ventilated hairpieces while Dave Matherly individually punched all the hair. A customized shoulder hump was also made to slope Hong's back and induce an old man's posture. Instead of creating back-of-the-hand appliances, however, makeup artist Ken Diaz came up with a formula for stipple - modified from Dick Smith's original recipe - which was applied in layers that, when dry, formed natural-looking wrinkles on Hong's own hand. False teeth, frequently used in such extreme makeups, were similarly eschewed. \"We rotted his teeth up a bit with tooth enamel, instead,\" explained Johnson. \"James Hong had so many lines we didn't want to impede his speech with false teeth. We also did not make contact lenses for him. John Carpenter felt that it would take away from the character. Lo Pan is not like a"],["33","Big Trouble in Little China","normal old-aged person; he's a demon that has kept himself alive for 2000 years by way of black magic. His eyes still needed to have intensity even though his face was shriveled. It also added credibility to the makeup because it was apparent that underneath all the latex, it was still James Hong.\" Two thousand-year-old demon or not, Lo Pan remains a youthful spirit seeking solid flesh; and until he achieves it, he must rely on his henchmen - a unique assortment of unearthly characters - to do his bidding and protect his labyrinthine, underground kingdom. Chief among Lo Pan's protectors are the Masters of Death, met earlier in the alley fight, also known as the Storms - aptly named Lightning, Thunder and Rain. The Storms literally descend on the scene in tandem - a green, glowing ball of energy wreaking havoc with the ceiling of a tenement where Jack Burton and Wang Chi have almost caught up with Miao Yin. Unfortunately, however, the hapless girl is snatched from our heroes' near-grasp by Lightning, who whisks her away via - what else? - a lightning bolt, up and out into the night. The initial effect of the trio entering through the ceiling was achieved by veteran floor effects supervisor Joseph Unsinn and crew. An explosion was rigged to rip apart the roof section, with the actors then flown in and out of the scene by wire lines. The challenging physical effect was enhanced with carefully planned interactive lighting, taking into consideration the degrees, direction and color of the light - and keeping in mind that there was ultimately going to be animation over it all. \"We also knew that animation was going to be moving in relation to Lightning's moves at the time,\" commented George Jenson, \"as well as reflecting his personality, For instance, for the scene where the script simply described, 'He takes a lightning bolt and rides it like a fireman's pole,' we would go beyond that and really give the stage direction personality, make it work with the scene. When Lightning rides that lightning bolt, it is full of all the energy that he's giving it. As he lets go and walks away, it stays there and becomes an inert kind of lightning, When he returns and grabs the bolt, his personality and strength bring it back to life. In other scenes, for example if Lightning were angry, his lightning had to reflect that. If he was trying to shoot someone with it, then it had to have a definite strength - as opposed to when he was walking down the hall and showing off for the girls by creating lightning on his fingertips.\" \"In the scene where Lightning lowers into the room on a lightning bolt,\" elaborated Garry Waller, \"they first rotoscoped him lowering and then went back and animated the bolt to his action so that it matched. A lot of life was added to the bolt in animation, too. Creating the lightning and the abundance of extraneous electricity actually constituted the bulk of our work. Three animators - Mauro Maressa, Peggy Regan and Glen Chaika - assumed the task of doing the animation and supplying the 'look' of the electricity, which was roughly based on an idea Richard Edlund had come up with. He thought that patterning the appearance of the lightning after Chinese calligraCINEFEX 28 • 33 PANAY PAN 1182"],["34","Big Trouble in Little China","34 * CINEFEX 28 15 of"],["35","Big Trouble in Little China","phy would be an interesting and fun way for the animators to use their own creativity. We had to keep it pretty toned down, though. The first tests we did turned out too stiff and cartoony, and John Carpenter wanted a more electric look. The lightning flashes come on screen and linger only a beat, but the calligraphy influence is in there although it's very subtle. But I think with its being in so many scenes throughout the film, people start to see and feel its presence.\" The lightning and electricity demanded by Big Trouble in Little China provided Boss Film with the unique opportunity - and time-consuming challenge - to once again pioneer special effects innovation. \"Whenever we get involved with special effects for a picture, it's very important to us to add something that hasn't been seen before,\" commented Richard Edlund. \"We never want to have the reputation for pulling shots out of stock from one movie and reusing them for another simply because we can easily repeat what we've already done. Not only would we get bored by that approach, but we feel it's our responsibility to any given film we're involved with to develop a special 'look' for that film that makes it stand out as something unique. In the case of Big Trouble, we really showcased the animation. Garry Waller even decided to use toplit art instead of the more typical bottomlit. We've done a lot of bottomlit animation with diffusion filters and so on; and that's generally the way it's done, because it's a little easier and perhaps a little cheaper to do it that way. But the final outcome is that you lose a lot of subtleties and texture by depending solely on artificial means to develop glows. We wound up airbrushing the glow into the lightning, which meant that each piece of art had to be done individually but which also gave the lightning a lot of nice crackle and glow - it truly looked electrical - while at the same time it had a softness and reality to it that made it genuinely look like lightning.\" Waller began by doing initial tests for the lightning in blackand-white, and from that point transformed them into color within the animation department - a departure from the routine method for animating electricity. \"It's traditional to take a black-and-white bolt and a penciled bolt on white paper, shoot it on black-and-white film and then give it to optical and let them diffuse, color and composite it into the scene. This time we did something different. We inked the lightning with white ink on the cel against black, diffused it and then put in an airbrush pass with another color. That way, we actually have a three-toned color to it. We also made the hotter parts of the bolt and the bolts that are closer to camera seem different. The hotter or closer bolts are brighter; and as they recede or go off into the distance, we forced the depth with a cooler color. At the front-most position, it's a bluish, bright-white and eventually gradates to a green as it cools off.\" Once the basic action was refined, the animator then added little tendrils, or threads, off the main bolt. At that point, the cels were given to assistants to do the 'in-betweening.' \"The next step was the inking,\" Waller continued. \"The animators During a confrontation between rival Chinese street gangs, the evil Lo Pan appears with his stormy minions. Chief among them is Lightning (James Pax) whose fingertips bristle with electricity. Under the supervision of Garry Waller, dozens of scenes were enhanced with animated lightning and electrical effects - carefully handdrawn as toplit art, then photographed with diffusion and enhanced separately with airbrushed glows. / Intent upon his abduction of Miao Yin (Suzee Pai), Lightning descends into a tenement building on a crackling bolt of lightning. / Technical animator Samuel Recinos at work on one of Boss Film's custom-built rotoscope stands. / Glen Chaika was one of three animators principally charged with establishing a look for the lightning effects. At Richard Edlund's suggestion, elements of Chinese calligraphy were subtly incorporated into the imagery. CINEFEX 28 • 35"],["36","Big Trouble in Little China","early stages of his laborious Designed and sculpted by Steve Johnson, the old-age appliances, a wispy beard and a foam rubber bald cap structure, veins and hair. / spirit in an ancient body, customary old-age contact lenses were not employed. And because Hong had so many lines to speak, speechimpeding false teeth were rejected in favor of simple Actor James Hong during and time-consuming transformation into the 2000-year-old Lo Pan. / makeup consisted of ten complete with cranial To suggest a still-young overlapping facial tooth enamel. 36 - CINEFEX 28 first lay the cel on the light box, then a piece of red acetate and finally some white paper on top of that. The red acetate made it easier to see the white ink on the cel. The inking was done with a really fine crow-quill pen because some of the threadlike electricity was extremely thin and called for a very thin line. That was critical, because once you go to color on top- or bottom-light against black, even a fine line becomes thick. After the drawings were inked, they were sent to the airbrushers who, on an overlay cel, airbrushed the thicker parts of the bolts where they were supposed to be hotter, closer or bigger. That was airbrushed against black. Then each layer was wedged - the airbrush against the bolts and vice versa to get complementary passes - and photographed. We actually did three passes - one pass of the airbrushed blue colors, and then two of the lightning, one white and one green because the finer stuff fell to a green color. It took about five hours to shoot one scene. Of course, it took weeks for the animation to get to that point, but it was kind of fun to add our own diffusion and try to do something a little different.\" \"Ordinarily, electricity is 'burned-in' with the diffusion added in optical,\" concurred optical effects supervisor Chris Regan. \"But Garry wanted to get away from that and control it at his end - which made things easy for me. He wanted to manipulate the arc absolutely so that all I had to do was simply lay it into the composite at one light. Because of the volume of electricity in the show, that was very important. Garry was very much the kingpin as far as the electricity for Big Trouble went. He did extensive design tests, basically for himself, in determining the look of the electricity - particularly in the way the animation was manipulated under his camera. It was really very subtle, but different from what is typically done. In effect, Garry did all of the subtle changes aside from the line manipulation done by the animators. So if you're looking at it frame by frame, it's always changing - the shape may not be constantly changing, but it is always changing subtly from greens to blues.\" After the Storms abduct Miao Yin, Jack Burton and Wang Chi regroup and decide to face Lo Pan head on. Posing as rather dubious telephone repairmen, they invade Lo Pan's fortress-like trading company only to find themselves swept immediately into some very deep trouble. An elevator takes them in over their heads to the Hell of Upside-Down Sinners - an underwater chamber filled with waterlogged corpses in varying degrees of decay. Steve Johnson, Craig Katon and Eric Fiedler came up with five upside-down animated 'zombies' for the sequence. The bodies for several of the zombies were made from the same mold -albeit individually modified - and each was given a different head. Additionally, plastic skeletons were cast inside, with the finished outer forms roughed up to reveal a glimpse of the bones. Jack and Wang are captured, of course, and brought before David Lo Pan himself. After a brief interrogation, Lo Pan orders Rain, by telephone, to have them properly dispatched. Lo Pan then decides to admire his green-eyed prize and magically transforms into his alternate identity. The youthful spirit warrior was also portrayed by actor Hong after his Lo Pan character underwent a filmic transformation. \"We handled Lo Pan's transformation in a really nontypical way,\" commented Johnson. \"You never actually see anything changing. It's different from the typical 'change-o' kind of stuff that's been done ever since American Werewolf with a mechanical deforming head. What happens in Big Trouble is after Lo Pan hangs up the phone, we cut to a close shot of him as he raises his hand in frame. His little fingernail grows longer and the hand begins to glow from within so you can see all the tendons and veins and the translucency of the flesh. It's as if there is an extremely high-powered flashlight behind it. Although we tried several techniques to achieve the effect of the growing fingernail, we ended up shooting the scene with the fingernail already long. The rotoscope department later covered it up with a little uneven matte and dissolved the nail out to its proper length. After Lo Pan takes his hand out of the frame, but before he gets his hand all the way out, his head starts to glow and you can see the skull up inside and the veins and so on. Mark Wilson did most of the work for that effect and it had some of the most difficult aspects in it that we had to overcome. The head had to start out looking just like Lo Pan, but it had to have enough of a trade-off quality so that we could light it from within. And we had to make it look translucent in order to see the veins and skull. We even had the brain pulse inside, which is kind of interesting. It starts out by glowing, so you can see there's really a skull in there. Finally, his eyes begin to glow. Animation later added headlightlike beams coming out of his eyes and mouth as well. That really enhanced the effect.\" Garry Waller animated Lo Pan's glowing eyes using the same streak photography technique as for the earlier scene in the alleyway. \"Doing the animation for the Lo Pan transformation sequence was really difficult,\" admitted Waller. \"I wanted the effect to be subtle enough so that the glow came only out of the eyes and the mouth and didn't cover up anything before we whited out. Besides that, the creature shop had done a really nice job on the head and I didn't want to spoil anything they had done by concealing it with animation. But the biggest problem was having to contend with the idiosyncrasies of the plate itself. It was actually a great shot because Lo Pan starts out looking towards camera and then turns totally in profile - but that made it difficult to do the flat art for the scene. I ended up having to force the look. I shot each piece separately, the mouth and the two eyes, so there were three elements. We rotoscoped the nose so that in profile, the one eye was eclipsed behind the nose matte. That way, it really looks as if it's in perspective.\" Internal lighting for the cable-operated Lo Pan dummy was achieved in a simple, yet highly effective way. \"We lit the head with lightbulbs,\" said Johnson. \"Just a couple of bulbs with rheostats so we could fade it up. We tried all sorts of different ways first, though. Mark Wilson did some tests using glow-juice"],["37","Big Trouble in Little China","CINEFEX 28 • 37"],["38","Big Trouble in Little China","38 * CINEFEX 28"],["39","Big Trouble in Little China","pumping through the veins. I wanted to put different colors of ooze inside the head and underneath the translucent Hot-Melt skin and then just dribble different colors over the skull and make weird things happen. But that was a nonrepeatable trick, and one thing really learned while working on the melting head for Fright Night was to do effects so that they can be repeated. That way you can make a couple of adjustments and do another take, which makes things so much easier - and you end up getting a better product. If you make it nonrepeatable and have a lot of complicated things happening, you might think that what you end up with is going to be a lot better, but it's not. You're simply not going to have the opportunity to shoot it enough times. If it takes a day to set the effect up again, the producers are going to say, 'Oh no, what we got yesterday was fine!\" Because of the inherent difficulties in photographing the uniquely illuminated head concurrent with the lighted hand of the Lo Pan dummy, the transformation was shot as two separate elements and later composited. It had been easy enough for the crew to light the dummy's head from within, but attempts at internally lighting the hand looked unconvincing. Thus the decision was made to light the hand externally and shoot it as a bluescreen element, thus permitting illumination bright enough to reveal the hand's skeletal structure without damaging the remainder of the illusion. Effects director of photography Bill Neil carefully coordinated his external lighting with first unit cinematographer Dean Cundey's to ensure a flawless transition. \"We shot the Lo Pan head and bust at Fox,\" explained Neil, \"and then composited the hand in later. We had to be particularly careful with the lighting because the rubber was transparent and eventually illuminated from within. It had to be lighted in a way that didn't prematurely reveal the transparency. We also did a little opaquing on the head. For instance, an ear that was backlit had a tendency to go transparent so we would opaque the back of it and other necessary areas to prevent revealing the transparency before the effect was noticed. The Lo Pan transformation was simply not something that could be done practical on the set. By shooting it as a separate element, we were able to manipulate part of the scene. We could then combine a live-action character with the rubber foreground character - something you couldn't do as a practical single element shot.\" Fully transformed, Lo Pan rises and glides through the wall and into an elaborate chamber where Miao Yin - dressed to the exotic nines in a costume designed by April Ferry - lies in hypnotic suspension. He appears to be corporeal, but when he tries to touch the girl, Lo Pan's spectral nature becomes apparent as his hand passes into Miao Yin, as if immersed in water. \"Miao Yin's floating effect was done practical by the first unit,\" explained Bill Neil. \"The early storyboards did have us doing a couple of shots in the beginning, where Boss would get involved in actually floating the characters, but it turned out to be unnecessary. So Joe Unsinn and his physical effects crew rigged the effect. It was basically just a support that was hidden by the character herself and attached through the wall to the rear. Most of Lo Pan's gliding was also done practically - James Hong simply stood on a dolly and was pushed along - but some of the shots were done at Boss. We shot the background plates at Fox, and then shot the actor against bluescreen with a gliding technique as was used earlier, ultimately compositing the elements together. Then with animation, we optically enhanced his walking through the wall. For the sequence where we first shot the plate of Miao Yin and then we shot Lo Pan with his hands against bluescreen. From there, animation took care The two separately photographed elements were put into precomp form and sent to the animation department where animator Mauro Maressa provided a 'wipe' effect. \"Mauro really became our wipe expert when he did the roto-animation for Poltergeist II,\" said Garry Waller. \"He was responsible for the effect of people walking through the seemingly-solid Kane at the shopping mall. The wipe for Big Trouble was done in a similar manner. We first determined where the top edge of Miao Yin's body plane was, and from that point on eliminated - or matted out - any part of Lo Pan's hands or arms that went any deeper into that plane. Because Lo Pan's hands were bluescreened, that automatically made a matte. Then Mauro Maressa made an animated matte of his hands breaking Miao Yin's body plane, which was printed transparent so we could see through it - sort of like a window rather than a solid door. Chris Regan did a great job printing the matte thin, so it wasn't totally black but rather gray. You could see through it. When Lo Pan travels through the wall, we introduced a water ripple into the composite. We filled a tank with water and Mat Beck, John Shriber and Peter Berman set up reflective white strips - so you could tell there was water moving - blew air onto it and shot it. Fading this element up and out as Lo Pan goes through really took the edge off the wipes as well.\" As Jack and Wang - blindfolded and bound in a couple of antique wheelchairs - struggle to escape, their cohorts Gracie Law and Margo Litzenberger have meanwhile intruded upon the trading company and gotten themselves captured. Our heroes come upon them - and a dozen or so captive Chinese girls - during the course of their escape and arrange a daring mass exodus through a narrow drainage pipe. Unfortunately, however, Miao Yin remains upstairs in a trance and Gracie - who, coincidentally, also possesses green eyes - is captured by one of Lo Pan's supernatural servants, the Chinese wildman. \"Although the Chinese wildman is another of Lo Pan's henchmen,\" explained George Jenson, \"this one has a mind of his own. He's in and out of the story strictly for shock-fright value. He was, in John Carpenter's early thinking, to be half-wolf and halfNosferatu. I went through probably a dozen drawings trying to develop the look that he wanted; and each time I drew the wolf's snout on the head, it just didn't seem to work. Then I found, A unique and nontraditional effect was employed to transform Lo Pan from ancient businessman to youthful spirit warrior. In a fairly tight shot, Lo Pan's little fingernail grows longer and his hand begins to glow from within. Then, as he lowers the hand, his head likewise begins to glow. Both hand and head - complete with internal bone structures - were animatronic elements shot separately and combined via bluescreen. / When the head-glow reaches full intensity, streams of animated light burst from the eyes and mouth flaring out the image. / Transformation complete, Lo Pan glides through a wall and into a chamber where Miao Yin lies in hypnotic suspension. As the magician reaches out to touch the green-eyed beauty, his spirit hand passes through her body - an effect achieved by shooting the hand against bluescreen and then treating the image with rotoscope wipes and partially transparent mattes. CINEFEX 28 • 39"],["40","Big Trouble in Little China","Another of Lo Pan's minions is the Chinese wildman - a centuries-old beast found roaming the labyrinthine Jenson design and a Steve Johnson sculpture, creature shop crew members Kevin Brennan and Theresa Burkett undergarment, sewing in appropriately-placed muscle padding and then covering it over with another spandex suit into which body hairs had been painstaking tied makes adjustments to the fiberglass underskull which featured a jaw mechanism sewers of Chinatown. Working from a George constructed the suit by starting with a spandex one at a time. / Burkett that opened and closed whenever actor Jim Kendelon opened and closed his own. 40 - CINEFEX 28 in a National Geographic magazine, a picture of a mummy that had a really incredible look about it. So I started to bring a little of that influence into the drawing and toned down the animal quality of the creature - made it fierce and more human. The result was a quality where he looked like a half-mummified, half-wild creature that had been wandering around the sewers of Chinatown for centuries, doomed to this life by some god. He even had shreds of old robes on him. That approach seemed satisfactory to John, so Steve Johnson took over from that point and did a three-dimensional sculpture. The wildman turned out to be another one of those half-amusing, halffrightening creatures.\" Casts were taken from the sculpture from which a wildman suit was rendered by Kevin Brennan and Theresa Burkett. \"We made what was basically a Greystoke suit,\" explained Johnson, \"using the same technology Rick Baker had worked out. The undersuit was made of an unusual kind of spandex with large, ventilated areas of mesh net so the suit could breathe a little more. Then on top of that, we added muscle padding which was sewn on at strategic points with elastic so that the muscles would slide over each other. That way, the actor wearing it had complete mobility. On top of the muscle layer we made a very tight-fitting spandex suit with hairs individually tied into it by Jack Bricker. He tied the hairs on one at a time, double knotting them throughout the entire body - as well as for several pairs of extra arms and feet - using different shades of reddishcolored hair. He did an incredible job. It's really the best way to go when doing a creature with hair because it allows the use of skin for the whole body - and the spandex suit was actually painted like skin. Theresa colored it with dyes, putting in a lot of mottling.\" The wildman's head was extended forward somewhat to minimize its resemblance to a human skull. A self-contained mechanism was rigged to work off the actor's jaw. When his jaw opened, the creature's mouth opened as well. \"The creature's lip could curl up and show teeth, too,\" elaborated Johnson, \"but that was about the extent of its capabilities. Dave Matherly did the mechanics for it and it worked out really nicely. We also made a really nice closeup head for which Makio Kida did the mechanics. There were probably twenty cables on that one - the ears pulled back, the eyes moved, they blinked, the brows worked just like the Greystoke brows, the tongue worked, the sides of the mouth pulled back, the cheeks puffed out. It was definitely going away from realism, but I've found that the most effective use of a mechanical head is to do broad movement. Even if it's not the best thing to do in all situations, it definitely worked in this case.\" Having found himself an unexpected dividend - two girls with green eyes - Lo Pan decides to marry both Miao Yin and Gracie. To rescue the captives, Jack and Wang join forces with Big Trouble's mystical, enigmatic tour bus driver, Egg Shen, and several members of a Chinese street gang. This impromptu SWAT team descends into the bowels of Chinatown and journeys through the sewer system. Their small band suffers an unpleas ant mishap, however, when a giant fishlike creature appears out of nowhere and snags one of the gang members. \"The sewer monster is the one true shock-cut we did for the film,\" said Johnson. \"It was the shot that was designed to get a jump out of the audience. Although it appeared briefly, it was a pretty difficult effect to rig. There was an actor inside the sewer monster, but the whole thing was built on a big track with a lot of wires to operate the arms and so on. Dave Kelsey and Ed Felix set up the rigging while Alex Felix devised the mechanics. Screaming Mad George sculpted the beast - it was roughly modeled after an angler fish - and supervised its assembly and operation. It was made of flexible foam with a steel understructure so we had to be extremely careful that when the monster shot out on the track it didn't actually hit the actor who was being attacked. If he had been hit accidently, it would have been as if he'd been hit by a car. As such, the shot had to be staged in a cheat - the actor had to be standing back far enough so there was no way the monster could catch him. At this point they cut away to a reaction shot. When we cut back to the man-eater, we used an interesting 'stand-in' for its victim. Screaming Mad George came up with some spring-mechanized legs that attached to the head of the actor playing the monster. The legs were very lightweight, so when he shook his head, the legs were activated, making it look as if the character was kicking around half-swallowed already.\" Shaken by this unfortunate incident, the stalwart troop continues to brave the dank tunnels of Lo Pan's underground caverns, ultimately finding themselves in a hallway known as the Spirit Path. Following them is another of Lo Pan's entourage, a guardian of sorts, capable of communicating with his master telepathically and possessing the bizarre appearance of a flying medicine ball-sized sphere dotted with eyes. The 'flying eye was designed by George Jenson, with further modification by Screaming Mad George, and ultimately sculpted by Jim Kagel. Screaming Mad George assembled and painted the complex creature. \"The other things we did for Big Trouble had problems that were difficult to solve,\" commented Steve Johnson, \"but they were just individual problems. Everything on the flying eye was a problem - basically because it was supposed to have thirty working eyes, make all sorts of faces and fly. From the beginning it was our biggest headache. Dave Kelsey did a lot of the actual design for the mechanism. And the thing we were primarily fighting, at least in the beginning, was how to puppeteer the eye without creating massive problems for the roto department. On Poltergeist II, the great beast had required something like twenty-five puppeteers to operate the dozens of rods and wires - all of which had to either be garbage-matted out or rotoscoped out.\" With the Poltergeist II experience fresh on everyone's mind, Richard Edlund and Bill Neil came up with an unusual and ingenious remedy. \"When we start working on an effect,\" commented Edlund, \"one of our first considerations is determining"],["41","Big Trouble in Little China","how we are going to shoot it - because when you design something, you've got to design it so it can be shot. And one of the problems in floating an object is first deciding how to float it and then how to get rid of the devices you use to float it. If it's just sitting on a pole, for instance, and very subtly moving around, you don't want to take a chance on picking up a breathing rotoscope line. We also take into consideration such factors as frontlight and backlight mattes or density mattes, which really don't work out well and still end up requiring a lot of rotoscope work. So, in the case of Big Trouble's flying eye, I figured that if we were to make a little donut-like bluescreen, the eye could be mounted right next to the screen. It seemed reasonable that if the eye had a hole in the back, the guys could then reach in to operate it yet it could still be surrounded by bluescreen material. Thaine Morris took care of the screen itself - he basically took a piece of blue screen, cut a hole in it and rigged it up so that the flying eye was mounted on a rod that went through a thirteen-inch hole in the screen. Then Bill Neil set the camera up in such a way that it 'floated' - which made the eymapear to be no tir inch fying eyes were made - to accommodate shots from both front and rear perspectives - and later reduced optically to the appropriate medicine ball specifications. \"Once we knew there were going to be shots from the back - with all the problems we had already - it seemed obvious that there was no way we could make one full sphere capable of doing everything,\" said Johnson. \"We tried to do as much direct rod operation as we could because the more direct the mechanism, the better. For example, the cheeks were actually on big fiberglass pieces that were operated straight out the back. For a pivot point we had a large plexiglass plate coming out at the back of the blue screen that we ran all the rods through. That way, the puppeteers could see through it and see what they were actually doing, as well as watch the monitor. It still took about twenty-four puppeteers, but nobody had to do any roto.\" The flying eye's cheeks were capable of movement, as were several of its 'major' eyes. \"It had two large mechanized eyes in the front that were really its major eyes for focus. Actually, they were the most highly mechanized function we did. They moved from side to side, up and down and any combination in between. They could roll, bug out, pull in and could also be set to operate independently. For instance, there's one shot where the flying eye gets stabbed in the forehead and its eyes cross and look right up at the knife. It also had to breathe, so we installed a system of bladders towards the bottom that could inflate and deflate. We put several other bladders in random places that just kept the skin bubbling a little bit - really subtly. We made a lot of the lesser eyes workable, but some we just made stageable - going on the principle that no one would notice - and we moved them slightly between shots. There was a little wire under the eyelid that could be pulled. Even the brows worked. It also had several eyes that were called CINEFEX 28 • 41"],["42","Big Trouble in Little China","42 • CINEFFY 28"],["43","Big Trouble in Little China","'feeling eyes.' They were located at the end of eyestalks, like a snail, and were attached to a big rod at the end of a funnelshaped collar which, in turn, was attached to the foam of the main flying eye body. They had mechanisms for a blink and eye movement; and when we moved the rod, all the flesh around each eye would move too. That added a lot of realism and organic movement to it. We could not only rotate the eyestalk a bit, we could twist it and push it out as well. They were all pretty much a four-cable mechanism and just came out in certain places. Only certain feeling eyes would move - they could stretch way out and around the back as if trying to peek over the front. We also used them for expression. When the eye was surprised, they would stick out like exclamation points; when it was scared, they would pull in. We made the eye's lower lip and jaw the same way we did the Onion Head ghost for Ghostbusters - which was some spring banding connected to the lower lip with a line coming out of the back. But once again, we were limited because the line couldn't come out from below - the optimum place to get the most movement out of it - it had to come straight out the back along with everything else. That made it really hard to maneuver.\" Background plates with the actors were shot on the set at Fox, with the eye later photographed on stage at Boss. \"The interesting thing about shooting the eye,\" commented Bill Neil, \"was trying to keep it animated with changes of expression, to keep it alive and also to keep it floating. The flying eye was locked off on a very rigid mount with all the puppeteers behind the blue screen. I floated the camera with pan and tilt just enough to keep the eye floating gently and to try and take the curse off the rigid mounting. It worked out pretty well, but it's really hard to judge sometimes when dealing with one element and knowing there is going to be another element down the road somewhere. I think, in retrospect, I was a little too conservative in the amount of movement I did use via the pan and tilt floating. If I had had the opportunity to reshoot the footage, I would have given the creature a little more movement so it would have had a greater sense of floating in the quick cutting of the sequence. Of course, these are judgmental things that you don't always hit right. Also, it was a difficult shot because there were so many people involved; but then a lot of rubber shots are difficult for that very reason. There was a lot of coordination involved; and with maybe ten or fifteen people operating ten or fifteen different aspects of the critter, it made things hard for everyone. Along with the problem of coordination was the reliability of the mechanics. In the radio-controlled units, for example, the batteries would get weak or there would be interference. Or one eye would go off looking in the wrong direction. And of course, with any bluescreen shot, you have to light in such a way as to not contaminate the blue screen with the lighting of the subject. In this case, the eye was only about seven inches away from the screen, which made lighting a real problem. For the most part, though, we'd solve the various setbacks with a lot of rehearsing, a lot of trial and error, mechanism repairs and so forth. I think we ended up with some pretty nice shots. The eye seems to come to life and has a real vitality on screen.\" Additional pan and tilt moves were created optically during the final phase of the flying eye composite on Boss Film's stateof-the-art Zap optical printer. \"The Zap printer was first built for use on Poltergeist II, \" explained Richard Edlund, \"and whenever you make something like that it's like constructing a violin - first you put it together and then you have to learn how to play it. In the case of the printer, we're still giving ourselves music lessons. The technique is evolving and we are continually discovering new capabilities and possibilities. We can now do a slow move-in on a shot, for instance - which we used to do as a locked-in, blocked-off shot - because we now have the ability to shoot it 65mm where it will stand a considerable blow-up without starting to look grainy. With the flying eye, we were able to add optical panning and floating on several shots by using the motion control aspects of the printer. So the software that was not quite finished for Poltergeist II was developed further on Big Trouble- it's an ongoing engineering process where we are continually refining things. The Zap printer and zoom lens are the biggest things we have done lately, technologically speaking, With those tools we can repeat a dozen times all the passes necessary for mattes, separations and the various elements required for a bluescreen shot - and do it perfectly so there's no color fringing at all. It has really freed up the optical cameraman to take care of whatever a shot dictates to advance the art of opticals. I think the Zap printer is the best in the world. I know it is.\" The flying eye follows Jack Burton and friends through the corridors and down a tiny elevator that opens upon the Great Arcade - an enormous and elaborate set designed by John Lloyd - where the marriage ceremony is about to commence. As the would-be rescuers regroup and contemplate their next move, the eye telepathically warns Lo Pan. Wang Chi draws a short sword and gives it to the flying eye right between the eyes - the beginning of some very fast-paced chaos culminating in the film's finale. For a closeup shot of the creature being stabbed, the large-sized eye was used in combination with an oversized sword made by Mark Segal. An additional, real medicine ball-sized eye - devoid of mechanisms - was made to be filmed flying off the end of a real-sized sword. The Great Arcade is the site of the great confrontation between the forces of good and evil - as personified by Egg Shen and Lo Pan - who do battle in an extraordinary and spectacular manner. \"The sequence wasn't actually in the script,\" said Edlund, \"but John Carpenter wanted to stage some sort of confrontation between Lo Pan and Egg Shen. He got together with George Jenson and George came up the idea to use ephemeral, spirit-like warriors that were conjured up by these two opposing forces. In this standoff between the enemies, Lo Pan crosses two of his long fingernails causing a beam of light to form into a warrior, while Egg pulls a magic stone from his bag of tricks A great deal of time and energy was expended on the sewer monster which appeared in only two quick cuts. Bearing strong resemblance to an angler fish, the man-eating creature was sculpted by Screaming Mad George, cast in foam over a steel understructure and mounted on tracks within the sewer set. / On cue, the cable-controlled creature - which also had an internal operator - darted out of a hole in the wall and gobbled up one of the lesser protagonists. / Making their way through Lo Pan's underground lair, Jack Burton (Kurt Russell) and his cohorts encounter a floating fleshball covered with eyes that telepathically alerts the ancient wizard to their presence. CINEFEX 28 • 43"],["44","Big Trouble in Little China","Designed by George Jenson and Screaming Mad George, the flying eye was rendered first in clay by Jim Kagel. To mechanics necessary to articulate its facial features - which included thirty working eyes - the floating sentry was constructed at twice its desired size. / The labor-intensive task of rotoscoping away support rods and actuating cables was eliminated entirely by positioning the flying eye in front of a small bluescreen with a hole cut into it. This simple innovation allowed cables to be fed out the back side of the eye and through the screen to a team of offcamera operators. Movement through the frame was achieved by floating the camera rather than the eye itself. / Hit by qunfire, the fluing eye drops to the ground and licks its wounds - a scene requiring both a puppet operator beneath the elevated set and several others concealed behind. / An obscene-looking tongue - actuated by hand from within - was designed with a retractable eyeball on the tip. / Boss Film technicians prepare for a scene in which the flying eye is dispatched accommodate the by a broadsword. 44 * CINEFEX 28 and projects a warrior as well.\" The warrior sequence was choreographed with live performers and photographed by Bill Neil. \"George Jenson did the original conceptual artwork for the sequence to give John Carpenter an idea of what it might look like. It was also helpful for everyone at Boss because it provided a common basis of understanding. Thaine Morris and his stage crew then set out to realize that on film. We went through a variety of tests and experiments until we got a look that started to play. We used some special incamera techniques to shoot the scene. First of all, the stage was heavily backlit and smoked to create light shards that were later enhanced by animation. We diffused the image somewhat with strategically-placed globs of vaseline in front of the lens to soften the image and make it more dreamlike. Then, to give the figures a lack of grounding - because the warriors were supposed to be doing battle in midair - we had smoke flowing over the tabletop they were actually performing on. To get the shards we wanted, the backlighting was just tremendous. We wound up using xenon projector arcs - just like the ones you'd find in the screening room at a theater - mounted on gimbals that tracked behind the characters. As the actors moved in and out in combat, we kept moving these huge arcs around trying always to keep the actors between the lens and the lamps. Thus if the actor moved two feet, the arc lamp had to move five feet. And it was quite a job to move those heavy lights around at performance level following what basically was dancelike movement. The lamp houses were hung in huge yokes. Thaine basically took the yokes off a Mole Richardson 10K, and made plates for the projector lamps and hung them in the yokes. We then put it on a boom arm so an operator was able to move this whole configuration around - it had a sort of neutral buoyancy. We could raise it up and swing in from side to side. Also, in front of that, there was a rotating color wedge that gave rotating color shards, which we counter-rotated to create the impression of conflict. We shot quite a bit of footage for the sequence, and there were enough good pieces to make the cut. The shoot really didn't seem all that impressive and I was a little nervous going in. But the film came out looking much better than I expected and everyone seemed happy with it.\" The warrior sequence also called for painstaking effort on the part of the rotoscope department. \"The two warriors began their action by rising up from a crouched position and then doing battle,\" explained rotoscope supervisor Annick Therrien. \"And because they were going to be matted into the composite so they appeared to be in midair, each of them had to be rotoscoped going through that action. We had to outline entirely each character, which was an extremely difficult thing to do because of the intense backlighting. It flared out in certain areas of their bodies, making it impossible to see that particular part of the body. So we did fake shapes - filling in parts that were missing by animating the shapes of the warriors as needed. It was very tricky to do because of all the nice detail in their costumes. We ended up rotoscoping every other frame and just"],["45","Big Trouble in Little China","CINEFEX 28 • 45"],["46","Big Trouble in Little China","46 - CINFFEX 28"],["47","Big Trouble in Little China","made up the shapes we weren't able to see because of the light. Sam Recinos, Rebecca Petrulli and Nick Freeman did the work, assisted by Maryanne Tucker. I had to have both a day shift and a night shift just to get those warrior shots done on time.\" The sequence was embellished further by the animation department which contributed sparks for the clashing swords - utilizing pin-blocking techniques - as well as enhancement for the warriors' auras by employing slitscan photography and moire diffusion. The background hubbub pales in significance as the forces do battle; but when Lo Pan and Egg Shen's confrontation draws to an end, the pandemonium resumes with gale-force fury. Lightning goes wild, blasting out bolts like a six-shooter and setting off a chain of events that eventually causes the Great Arcade to crumble. In one amazing take, John Lloyd's set was laid waste by floor effects supervisor Joe Unsinn and his principal crew members Stanley Amborn, James fredburg, Bruce Robles and David Kirk. \"On the set, there was a huge Buddhalike idol,\" explained Unsinn. \"It was made of carved styrofoam, with a hollow interior, and measured about twenty feet high and thirteen feet wide. At one point during the sequence, it was our job to fly the Rain character into the belly of the idol. Naturally, that does Rain in, but it also causes the idol to collapse and topple down the stairs. We first filled the idol with both styrofoam and pyrocell rocks - which were painted to glow fieryred - and rigged our equipment and some pyrotechnics in through the back of the idol. We set it off on cue - it was not prescored in any way, it was actually sheared apart by the explosive material that we used. Next we had to explode the arms and legs, light up the head and eyes and then, again on cue, begin to have the idol fall forward while the rest of its arms exploded off. On the same set there was an escalator that ascended into the mouth of a giant, neon-lit skull. My crew and I were responsible for the fires inside of it and also for the jaws which opened and closed - and we also did some pyrotechnic work where we blew out one side of the jaw.\" Jack and Gracie escape as Egg Shen battles a score of Lo Pan's warriors; but, unfortunately, Miao Yin remains in the maniac's clutches long enough for the wedding ceremony to be completed. Lo Pan thus becomes flesh. As Thunder is about to complete the ancient bargain with Ching-ti and throttle the innocent Chinese girl, Jack Burton strolls on the scene in time to intervene. Lo Pan, in his long-desired mortal form, is ultimately vulnerable to mortality and after receiving Jack's survival knife in his forehead - a bulls-eye hit to the amazement of his compatriots - Little China's biggest trouble dies in an earth-shaking swan song. Our heroes' problems, however, are not yet over. They still have Lo Pan's remaining henchmen to contend with - most immediately, a furious-to-the-burstingpoint Thunder. \"Thunder is really, really outraged by Lo Pan's death,\" explained Edlund, \"so outraged, in fact, that his head swells and steam comes out of his ears and nose. He begins to inflate, and ultimately inflates into this enormous Thunder balloon. As Jack, Gracie and the others run out of the room, Thunder explodes and parts of him go flying out into the hallway - but it's not gory at all, it's really very funny.\" Although originally intended to begin with a transformation makeup on actor Carter Wong, the effect of Thunder's ultimate temper tantrum utilized a dummy likeness of the actor instead. \"The transformation makeup just didn't work,\" admitted Johnson, \"because it didn't look enough like Carter. One of the problems with it was that we had established his real face too much. We had a couple of shots of him getting angrier and angrier and making a lot of expressions, and then we cut away and then back to the makeup - there was no way it could hold up in a situation like that. Even though they decided not to use the first-stage makeup we'd worked on, I was very happy they as very happy n the decided to fix that sequence. It was the only sequence in the whole film that I wasn't really thrilled with. And the changes made a lot of sense, too. Richard Edlund had a big hand in boarding the insert shots and he's always been against the typical kind of effects transformation sequences that go from one shot to a cutaway and then back to find something changed in the first shot. So we decided to do a dummy head instead. Just a completely false head. We got Carter back in, did a life casting - with an expression - and we installed bladders like we did with the other head; so we were able to start out with absolutely no additions on it, it was just his face there. And it's a quick-enough cut so you'll never realize it's not really him. Dave Matherly did most of the work on that one while Kevin Brennan took care of the extra inflating hand and feet. For cartoon quality, Dave Kelsey rigged it with freon gas so that steam blew out of the nostrils and ears. It worked really nicely. And from that point it just continued inflating. Bladders were placed in basically the same areas as the first-stage makeup, but they were larger. We also saw a bit of shoulders in that shot, so we had to have the character's costume on the dummy. The head had very little animation - it moved somewhat, the rodoperated jaw opened and the eyes bugged out.\" At that point, the camera cut away to a reaction shot and then back to Thunder's full body. \"Eric Fiedler and Theresa Burkett were responsible for that version,\" Johnson continued. \"It was based on a spandex suit with weather balloons encased in several spandex pockets, and Mark Segal painted the whole body like skin. It got really enormous. The costume had to be rigged a little bit to tear away, too, because the body got so big it was obvious something had to give. We sculpted fat hands and feet for it, as well. We also had to make a third head for the final shot because it was obvious that Thunder should be inflated even further. So we just took the second-stage skin and added some more bladders, inflated it a little more and spiked out his hair. It looked good, and everybody really liked it on the set. After we did a couple takes, John Carpenter started egging me on to pop it, to go as far as we could inflating it and just see what happened. We let it go pretty far - much farther than we had in any of the tests - but at the last minute, I just Lo Pan has a worthy adversary in Egg Shen, an ersatz tour bus driver whose magical powers rival his own. Together they square off in the Great Arcade, conjuring up spirit warriors that engage each other in battle. The ephemeral images were actually liveaction performers photographed on a smokefilled stage with intense backlighting from xenon arcs. In front of the arcs - mounted on gimbal rigs for maximum maneuverability - were rotating color gels. Sparks and slitscan enhancement were provided by the animation department and a major rotoscoping effort was required to generate the mattes needed to effectively composite the images. / During the melee, Lightning tosses out bolts which ultimately cause the destruction of the Great Arcade. / In addition to animated lightning and optical explosions, the battle sequence included major pyrotechnic effects engineered by physical effects supervisor Joe Unsinn. CINEFEX 28 • 47"],["48","Big Trouble in Little China","Outraged by Lo Pan's death, Thunder's previously-human form swells up to zeppelin explodes. / Steve Johnson tests the eye mechanism built into the headpiece of the inflatable Thunder. / The spandex and equipped with strategically placed weather balloons and bladders fed by hoses in the back. / John mountainous character prior to inflation. / To impart a figure was rigged with freon gas that looked like steam when expelled through its proportions and then suit itself was made of compressed air through Carpenter examines the cartoonish quality, the nose and ears. 48 * CINEFEX 28 figured that was close enough. I wanted to be sure we had something to retake with in case the gate jumped or some other disaster fell - so we never did break it. \"Because the idea of Thunder expanding is absurd to begin with, it seemed one of the few things we could really have fun with, find a lot of humor in - especially since the script stressed that it was supposed to be non-gory. Since he expands and keeps on expanding, you think of him as a balloon. Ultimately he gets so large, he explodes. In that scene, you see a lot of people running out of the room and as they run out, you also see all these 'Thunder parts' fly out. I came up with a couple of funny ideas for that one. I wanted to make a really thin skin on his head, put a big balloon inside, inflate it a lot and then just let it go - have it fly around the room like balloons do when they're released. I thought it would have been great to see his head whizzing around. Well, John liked the idea but it just didn't work out. We couldn't make a light-enough head and the effect started getting complicated with wires and so on. The other thing I thought of was having the guts and dismembered parts that go by camera in party balloon colors. So we made all these latex Hot-Melt pieces in brilliant pinks, reds, blues and greens, and I went to John and said, I have another idea, but you're probably going to think it's ridiculous.' He said: 'Ridiculous? Are you kidding? Try it on me!' So I explained the idea and dumped all the colored latex pieces on the ground for him to see. He looked at them for a minute, then turned to me and said, You're right, that's ridiculous.' We ended up making Thunder's parts a little more pastel.\" With two of the Storms down and one to go, Lightning hounds our heroes as the sole surviving fury, blasting whatever floors and ceilings stand in his way. Finally, a bolt aimed at Egg Shen backfires when the old man raises a reflective fan as a shield, causing the electricity to ricochet and zap Lightning instead. Out of big trouble at last, Wang and Miao Yin are happily united - Jack and Gracie are not - and all seems to end well for the entire company, at least for the time being. In a unique way, the fast-paced, nonstop action of the Big Trouble storyline mirrored the fast-paced, nonstop production schedule imposed on the $19 million film - which was released almost a year to date from the first time John Carpenter read the screenplay, \"Whether it's big budget or low budget, it's always hard to make a film,\" Carpenter concluded. \"It's all the same. On low budget movies, when you have little money, you often have to stretch things in order to cover your bases and make sure you have a film. You would think that with a big budget film you would have all kinds of luxuries. Not so. It's really about the same. The problem with Big Trouble was that we had a very short schedule for an enormously complex film. Unfortunately, the studios nowadays love to cut down your postproduction time; they love to cut it down to three or four months. And on a film the size of Big Trouble, that made things very hard. So we made this movie an awful lot like a low budget film - just running gun.\""],["49","Big Trouble in Little China","CINEFEX 28 • 49"],["50","Short Circuit","Building the Body Electric"],["51","Short Circuit","'Star quality,' it has been said, is difficult to define but easy to recognize. It is partly a look, partly a personality that by virtue of its perfection or oddness immediately captures the attention of millions, Eastwood, Streep, Newman and Redford all have it and - judging by the popular success of director John Badham's Short Circuit - a six-foot-tall robot named Number 5 has it too. He is witty, endearingly naive, intelligent, cute, potentially dangerous, yet a pacifist - and he singlehandedly steals the show. Well, not quite single-handedly. For in reality, Number 5 is the result of a collaboration of talented writers, an innovative director, a well-respected designer, a team of multifaceted puppeteers and more than forty mechanical effects experts. The birth' of Number 5 - which in the film is the miraculous consequence of a lightning bolt - was actually an engineering and design feat almost no less miraculous. Short Circuit chronicles the adventures of a highly sophisticated, technologically-advanced, $11 million robot that is part of a series designed to be lethal combat weapons. Though his inventor, Dr. Newton Crosby (Steve Guttenberg), contends that the robots don't think or feel and are mere machines that 'just run programs, an act of nature inexplicably turns robot number five into a thinking, feeling and independent creature. Number 5 is alive - and Short Circuit is off and running. Inadvertently escaping the confines of the Nova Robotics plant that manufactured him, Number 5 finds himself out in a world he does not understand - nothing in his computer banks has prepared him for cows or gas stations or pretty young girls. He meets Stephanie Speck (Ally Sheedy) - one such pretty young girl with a propensity for taking in strays - and delightedly soaks up 'input' in the form of books and television. Meanwhile, military personnel are frantically searching for their multimillion dollar weapon. Once convinced that Number 5 is indeed alive, Stephanie tries to protect him from the military brass and scientists who believe him to be only a malfunctioning, dangerous piece of machinery. Written by S.S. Wilson and Brent Maddock, the script for Short Circuit was an immediate 'hot' property, \"David Foster and Larry Turman have been sending me scripts for years,\" John Badham admitted, \"and I kept sending them back, saying no. But they sent me Short Circuit and said, 'We're not going to mess with you this time - we're sending this script out to four other directors so you'd better read it quick.' I started reading it right away and I thought it was really good. I was very excited about it because the character of the robot was so much fun and so special. So, this time, I called Foster and Turman right Short Circuit photographs copyright © 1986 by Tri-Star Pictures and PSO Presentations. Production unit still photography by Bruce McBroom. Additional photography courtesy Eric Allard. Title page illustration by Syd Mead. Special thanks to Sharon Braverman. back. We set up a lunch meeting and it was done.\" The character of the robot that so charmed John Badham had been many drafts in the making. Indirectly, in fact, it had been many years in the making. Cowriter Steve Wilson had been fascinated with robots since his childhood. Intensely interested in animation, but stymied by his own inability to draw, Wilson began exploring other options. \"I turned to stop-motion animation,\" Wilson recalled, \"but I couldn't really sculpt very well either. My dinosaurs didn't look anything like anybody else's dinosaurs. So I started animating anything that had moving parts on it and, over the years, I developed a knack for putting life and character into things that were totally nonanthropomorphic. A robot, of course, would be a perfect device for my particular talents and I pitched robots to the educational film market for years. I was finally allowed to do 'Library Report' which featured a talking robot explaining how to use the library. I built the robot out of junk parts from surplus stores and it was all done with stop-motion. The film was a tremendous hit in the educational market and, in my mind anyway, that robot was the forerunner of Number 5.\" Deciding it was time to tackle another screenplay - and writing only on 'spec' - Wilson teamed with Brent Maddock and the two began creating a story with a robot as its central character. \"I envisioned it as a low-budget movie,\" Wilson commented, \"with me doing the stop-motion animation for the robot. The germ of the idea was that the robot was alive - I knew that from day one - but I didn't know how that was to come about. My first thought was that it could be a person who had been mechanized until only his brain remained intact, but Brent threw a fit about that because it was so grisly. So we started with the problem of how a robot could be alive and then actually die if it got disassembled. We thought of aliens coming down and zapping the robot alive. We thought of aliens coming down and they are machines and they are alive. Finally, we went back to the old lightning bolt gag, even though we didn't want to. We never could come up with anything else that was as clean. We didn't want the story to get bogged down with how Number 5 comes alive. The point is, he comes to life; and we hoped the audience would buy that so we could get on with the Though hardly the novice writers they have been described - both Maddock and Wilson had been making their living as writers for ten years and the Short Circuit script was not an assignment for a UCLA class as has been reported - they were understandably excited that the script was met with such enthusiasm. The low-budget, stop-motion animated film they had envisioned was now in the hands of a major studio and a highpowered director. With Badham at the helm, the notion of using stop-motion animation to achieve the robot effects was one of the first things to go. \"There was a strong prejudice against using stop-motion for this project, from the very beginning,\" article by said Wilson. \"Even before Badham came on, David Foster was Jody Duncan Shay against the idea. He had been one of the coproducers on Cave-"],["52","Short Circuit","rehearses his performers - and Brent Maddock, the film opens with the unveiling of a new line of sophisticated, unaccountably comes to life curiosity and a blossoming sense of self-preservation. / At his All Effects facility in North Hollywood, Eric Allard man crew to engineer and build the robot action props - discusses construction consultant Philip Harrison Jones. / Although five robots would be featured - with prominent - twenty-seven full and partial robots were Director John Badham both live and mechanical - during principal photography on Short Circuit. Derived from a screenplay by S.S. Wilson laser-equipped military robots, one of which is struck by lightning and with an abundance of - who assembled a fiftytechniques with visual and co-producer Dennis Number 5 being most required to fulfill the demands of the script. 52 * CINEFEX 28 man and he felt they'd really gotten burned on the stop-motion effects. I was running around with my demo tapes of the original educational film from which Number 5 grew, showing everybody what the potential was. And we'd written a number of scenes specifically to exploit the capabilities of stop-motion - like one where Number 5 did some fancy shuffling and dealing with a deck of cards. But, understandably, Badham didn't want to turn over the central character of his film to postproduction. He didn't want to be on the set thinking, 'Gee, I hope they do a good job with my main character in this scene.\" Having rejected the concept of using stop-motion animation to create his 'star, John Badham began talking to various effects facilities - among them, Apogee, ILM and the Carlo Rambaldi organization. Even actual computerized robot manufacturers were approached, though that option was quickly discarded. \"We realized that we were going to have to use every movie trick in the book to actualize this robot,\" explained Badham. \"There was no way we were going to be successful if we tried using real state-of-the-art robotics - you can really get tangled up in that sort of technology. Sometimes relying on old rubber band and paper clip techniques is the best thing to do.\" Badham was fortunate in finding Eric Allard, a young mechanical effects wizard who had already tackled the difficulties in building a robot for a Showscan film called Let's Go. \"The 'Pal' robot featured in Let's Go had actually been designed by Dan Goozee,\" Allard related, \"and was operated by a rodpuppetry technique that Doug Trumbull had come up with. Like Number 5, it had a lot of character and expression in its face. After John Badham and his group saw Let's Go- which was part of a robotics exhibit at the Tsukuba Expo in Japan - they gave me a call. I got the script on a Thursday or Friday and over the weekend I came up with a proposal. I broke down each scene and each page and noted what kind of robot prop I thought they'd need. I told them the last thing they wanted was a real robot, which I think is what sold them on my concept. A real robot has no place on a sound stage. I had worked on Brainstorm which had some industrial robots and they proved to be real time-consuming to shoot - reprogramming them for just a slight artistic change was a real hassle. My concept would give the director spontaneous input - just like working with an actor.\" Principal photography was scheduled to begin in just over five months, leaving Allard with a ridiculously short amount of time in which to build a character that had yet to be designed. The schedule was tightened further by the fact that Badham was hoping for a month of testing and rehearsal with the robot before shooting commenced. Aware of the complexity of the undertaking, Badham needed a designer with more than just a fanciful grasp of hardware and technology. \"I wanted this robot to take us a hundred years into the future,\" Badham said, \"and that was a very tall order. In the first place, Number 5 was a foot soldier. What kind of thing is going to do that job? It had to have some kind of firepower, some ability to move around on all sorts of terrains. It had to be capable of handling situations you would send a human being into, yet invulnerable enough to survive warfare. You also had to be able to get some expression and emotion and feeling out of this guy because it was essential that the audience empathize with him. He couldn't be the faceless, anonymous character in 2001. And he couldn't be what we had in Wargames - a slightly devilish computer that was really just blinking lights on a box. He had to be a person of sorts, yet designed so that it was apparent that he wasn't really just a person in a suit.\" To fill his 'tall order,' Badham approached Syd Mead, Mead was not only an experienced designer in the film industry - having done work for Star Trek, Blade Runner and 2010- he was also a prominent industrial designer. \"What made this project so fascinating,\" said Mead, \"was coming up with a robot that was a dramatic character. In effect, we had to create a robot that could satisfy all the same story and dramatic requirements as a live actor yet within considerable mechanical limitations and time constraints.\" Before beginning his design work, Mead was sent to the Tsukuba exhibition where a number of stateof-the-art, ambulatory robots were on display. \"The thing that impressed me from what I saw in Japan was how slowly real robots move. Number 5 had to move in choreographed sync with real actors, which meant a fairly quick reaction time that real robots just don't have, We are a long way from Number 5 in real-life robotics. I was, however, influenced by what I saw there and some of the linkages and joints and rotational details of the real robots found their way into Number 5's design. The biggest influence was an organ-playing robot which was fixed to a piano bench and actually scanned sheet music with its optical system. I was interested in that one because it had two camera eyes with bifocal vision and it had some expression on its face - which was, of course, a primary concern in designing Number 5.\" Once back from Japan, Mead began daily meetings with John Badham, visual consultant Philip Harrison and Eric Allard, who had been included for his own design input and to ensure that the interesting ideas being put down on paper could realistically be built. The meetings would continue for an intensive six weeks of design give-and-take. \"Some of those meetings seemed to go on forever,\" Mead admitted. \"The four of us would have a sort of 'drawing' discussion, everyone scratching things out on paper; then I would take home my notes from that discussion and do specific, detailed drawings. From those drawings we'd decide if we wanted to include a certain characteristic or what changes needed to be made. It was a very collaborative process.\" One of the first priorities was deciding how Number 5 would move. \"Since the robot had to move very fast and be quite agile, I thought wheels might be the way to go about it. I proposed a three-wheel system, like a tricycle. The wheels would be mounted on articulated suspension legs that would allow you to bring the wheels together so the robot could stand up. It was very spindly looking and I liked it very much, but it"],["53","Short Circuit","CINEFEX 28 • 53"],["54","Short Circuit","54 • CINEFEX 28"],["55","Short Circuit","wasn't suitable for Eric's mechanical requirements. In order for the machines to actually move around on their own power, they would have to have pretty hefty batteries on board and there was no place in this design to put those batteries. All of the other bodily parts were going to be very thin and spindly so that the audience would know that it was a real machine with nobody hiding inside of it. We had to have someplace where Eric could put all the mechanical stuff, so we went with tracks which gave us a good heavy base. It worked dramatically too because tracks enforced the idea that this was a military machine.\" Number 5 also required some flexibility in height with the ability to squat down into a 'combat' position and raise up to an eye-to-eye level with his human costars. \"To get that scissor action required articulation of the main stub of his body at the bottom,\" Mead continued. \"It could move back towards the rear, making it shorter; then, as it moved forward it would raise up by about a foot. The neck also periscoped up and down. Another thing I did with his body was to give him a dropped shoulder look so that it wasn't exactly symmetrical. His laser was off-center on one side and that added an odd asymmetric accent that made it look almost endearingly clumsy or awkward. It was very important that it not just look like an armature for a piece of sculpture that was later going to be covered up with something. It had to look like a highly specialized machine which, because of its function, just happened to look like a human being.\" One of Number 5's nonhuman characteristics was his third arm, called the tool arm. The tool arm had been the writers' invention and, in the script, had been capable of many functions similar to the James Bond gadgetry. Practical considerations made a simplification of the arm a necessity, however, and in the final design it is neatly folded into his backside, rarely seen or used. As the key to his personality, a great deal of the allotted design time was spent on Number 5's face and head. \"Originally, I proposed that the face and head not be totally anthropomorphic,\" noted Mead. \"I wanted it to be more machinelike and have a happenstance resemblance. It's strange how quickly we perceive a face out of three dots. You get 'face' very rapidly when you are playing around with mechanical detail and I wanted to stay away from that. So I kept coming up with these sketches that were very machinelike and logical for what a thing like this would have to do. But as we progressed, John decided he wanted two matching eyes and a mouth - more of a symmetrical face.\" Most of the robot's 'acting' ability would come from his expressive and flexible eyes and eyeflaps. \"His eye flaps acted as eyebrows and they were originally the flash shields that you would have on a sophisticated scanning device to protect it against sudden bursts of light - anything from nuclear explosion to blinding flashlights or flares. They become his eyebrows in the course of the film because they get damaged and they flop a little bit and your mind makes that immediate connection. For the actual eyes, I had preferred a very complicated optical system that would occur behind the lens but they Several weeks of the short preproduction schedule were devoted to conceptualizing the all-important robot. Principal designer was Syd Mead - working in close collaboration with Eric Allard, Philip Harrison and John Badham. Allard demonstrates for Harrison and Mead a foam-core study mockup fashioned prior to the actual construction phase. / Design interpreter Chris Ross prepares a vacuform pattem needed to produce panel coverings for the radio-control versions of the robot. / Richard Hill and Robert Horvatich install a completed arm mechanism on one of the rod- and cablecontrolled puppets developed primarily for closeups. / Although the basic chassis was aluminum, other lightweight materials were used whenever possible. Adam Gelbart files out eye sockets in an epoxy resin headpiece. / During assembly, Rick Hill lubricates one of the articulated hands designed by Allard. CINEFEX 28 • 55"],["56","Short Circuit","Eric Allard puts a prototype workout for John Badham addition to moving forward and backward, the finished artifact could rotate at its crouched to upright position and bring its laser weapon to bear. In all, eight of the full-size, radio-control robots operators to produce its full Blaney and Carl Johnson - members of Tony Urbano's practice with one of the rodand cable-actuated variants. and finger manipulations robot through its paces during a preliminary and the producers. In base and neck, articulate both arms, shift from a were fabricated - each requiring nine external range of functions. / Tim puppeteering team - Two sets of insert arms were constructed and employed whenever especially intricate hand were required. 56 * CINEFEX 28 eventually used a mechanism that Eric came up with. It was there and available and it was more out in the open - you could actually see the mechanism sliding back and forth and up and down.\" The fact that the robots were supposedly built for military purposes was another important consideration in the design process. \"The laser was mounted on the back to resemble the way soldiers carry guns on their backs when they aren't using them,\" Mead explained. \"They are back out of the way but can be instantly brought into position when needed. And it comes into position right by the head so it's in line with the robot's scanning system - a very logical position for the weapon to be in. Another design element that we hoped would make the robot believable as a military weapon was its ability to crouch down into a lower silhouette as if in combat. Also, the treads denoted military use and were covered with shrouds. Philip Harrison did a delightful series of 'attitude poses' in camouflage colors just to get us into the spirit of the whole military thing. There was some talk at one point of painting them - for the purpose of the opening military demonstration - in arctic white and jungle camouflage colors to demonstrate that they were suited to all environments, but that idea was eventually dropped.\" Though Eric Allard had been in on the process from the beginning, the prospect of actually bringing Number 5's ap proved design into three-dimensional reality was an overwhelming one. \"Syd comes up with wonderful stuff,\" Allard asserted, \"but because he is about fifty years ahead of the rest of us, there are problems automatically built in. With every new step in the design, more problems were introduced. I had been included in the design phase, partly to keep everything under control mechanically, but I figured this movie was my big shot and I just wanted to go for it. I didn't ever want to say, 'No, we can't do that.' By the time the design was finalized, I looked at it and Mas that: Bon my G thought, 'Oh my Godl' The design had progressed from something that would have been fairly easy to build to something that was just about impossible. But I had made a lot of claims and promises and I just had to do it.\" Allard had three-and-a-half months in which to complete the various action props that collectively would become Number 5. \"There were certain things in Syd Mead's drawings that I realized right away wouldn't really work,\" Allard commented. \"The width of the tracks, for instance, scaled up to real size, wouldn't fit through doors. Also, the final drawing didn't have a place for him to rotate at the waist. I was pushing for a very low base rotation - which would have been simpler to build - but in the interest of anthropomorphizing him, they wanted the rotation to be at the waist. That was very difficult because it meant that his whole upper torso had to meet at one little rotation point at the waist and still function. I also knew the neck would be a problem. It was supposed to be able to go back and forth while the head remained parallel to the floor. But the neck joint, as shown on the drawing, couldn't possibly support the weight of the head. The design also gave us no place to put motors to work the robot's arms. A real robot with functioning arms would have a big power pack at the elbow - or a power unit under a table somewhere, which would limit its mobility. Arm movement was obviously very important if the robot was going to gesture convincingly. Finally, the design called for Number 5 to be able to adjust his height all the way from a squatting position of only four feet to a maximum height of seven feet. Geometrically that just wasn't practical. We had to settle for forty-seven inches as its maximum low and six-anda-half feet as its maximum height. Those were just some of the initial problems - the all-consuming problem, of course, was that this thing had to do such an amazing variety of things.\" Allard had tackled the amazing range of Number 5's capabilities in the original proposal he had submitted to John Badham. Breaking down each of the robot's many scenes, he had calculated that no less than twenty-seven action props would be required to perform all of the robot's astounding functions. \"What I called the Type 1' robot was the main radio-controlled, full-size unit that would be used for long shots,\" Allard explained. \"There were eight of those and they were as fullyarticulated as possible, but without a great deal of detail. As many as nine operators were required to articulate the Type 1 robots, which had forward and reverse movement with variable speed, base and neck rotation, arm and elbow articulation and the laser functions. This unit would establish that Number 5 could move under his own power and at least suggest that he could do everything else.\" The bulk of Number 5's more complicated actions would be performed by waist-up rod puppets mounted either on dollies which could be pushed or a teeter-totter rig which could raise and lower them. \"The puppets basically had all the same functions as the remote control robot, except that they were rodactuated and they were only from the waist up. The only motorized articulation they had was the bend at the waist, the upper boom movement back and forth, and the sort of turkey neck movement of the head. Everything else was puppeteered, either by cable or hydraulic systems.\" Since Number 5 would be required to fix a martini, make breakfast and generally gesture in a realistic way, the puppet's arms and hands were of utmost importance. \"The arms were designed so that the operator actually put his hands into them - attached at the elbows -and then manipulated pull rings that caused the puppet's hands to respond according to specific movements made by the puppeteers.\" Insert arms were also built to perform the more complicated manipulations. \"We built two sets of insert arms - two rights and two lefts. They were used in the beginning of the film where there are closeup shots of the robots mixing drinks and again in the same sequence where they assemble a model airplane - a bit that was later cut. They were also used when Number 5 pulls the tablecloth off the table and for shots of his arm coming out of the truck and adjusting the rear-view mirror. Anytime it was just a hand and an arm"],["57","Short Circuit","doing detailed work, we used the insert arms. In other scenes, we'd set up the robot with no arms behind a table, for example, and then have a guy operating an insert arm on either side of him. As long as the shot was framed in a certain way, it looked perfectly natural.\" To allow for optimum flexibility during shooting, each of Number 5's parts was made to fit any version of the robot - radio controlled, puppet or dummy. \"Everything was made so you could take any part of any robot and put it on any other robot. I knew it would be essential when we actually got out there and started shooting. It doesn't matter how well you plan these things; when you get on the set with a creative director like John Badham, things are going to come up and you have to be flexible enough to accommodate him.\" Because of this flexibility, only five heads were actually needed, each able to attach to any type of robot. \"Three of the heads were capable of sixteen separate functions, and we ended up with more than twenty servos just in the heads alone. The eyes could pan and tilt and there was an iris which worked inside the lens. They had eyebrows that were pretty complex, too. They could flap, either together or independently, and the whole assembly tilted back. The eyes also had louvers on the sides that opened and closed.\" The head also included a voice module - a panel of lights the audience could relate to as the source of Number 5's voice. \"We used a wireless remote microphone that Tim Blaney - who was the head operator and the voice of Number 5 - would speak into. As he did, the lights on the voice module would start to dance. It was like a color organ - the louder he talked, the more lights would come on.\" The remaining two heads were simpler versions, each with their own special function to perform. \"One head was intended just for the scene where Stephanie first sees Number 5 in her truck and she thinks it's an alien. That one had huge projector bulbs in its eyes and, though it did have some upper eye flap functions, it didn't have the atmospheric sensors or most of the other eye movements. The fifth head was the 'bug-eye' head. Its eyes were rigged so they could actually pop out. It was a subtle effect and we ended up using it only a couple of times - for the scene where Number 5 sees Stephanie naked and also when he chases the grasshopper.\" Like all stars, Number 5 required stunt doubles to take over when the action got too rough. \"We built eight stunt robots that had little or no articulation. A couple of them had some lower boom-type action so we could position them specifically for a shot, but there were no radio controlled functions on any of them. They were going to take a lot of abuse, so there was no point in putting delicate mechanisms in them.\" Even the heads were dummies. These were to be used for shots of him falling off the truck and falling into the chicken coop, among others. Allard and his crew also built eight half-scale miniatures with little parachutes on them for a scene in which Number 5 is catapulted off a bridge. \"At the very beginning of the project,\" Allard recalled, \"Tom Pahk, Rick Hill and I built a full-scale, foam-core mockup of the CINEFEX 28 1 57"],["58","Short Circuit","58 - CINEFEX 28"],["59","Short Circuit","robot. Tom and Chris Ross then built the patterns. Since they had worked with Syd Mead on two other films, they were very good at interpreting his drawings. The mockup was basically to show the size of the robot in relation to everything else - to make sure it could get in and out of vehicles and that type of thing. It was also to work out some of the basic geometry for the large body functions - the boom going up and down, where the actuator should be placed, the parallelograms for keeping the upper torso parallel to the floor. As soon as the mockup was finished, Tom Pahk began making parts. Dave Shwartz set up the mold shop - we realized by that time that we were going to need over fifty different molds for this thing. Ron Gress did the painting, which was a major task in itself. Chris Ross worked on concepts for the head - which at that point still wasn't very clearly defined - so he spent the first six weeks just on the face. I'd had an idea for a multiple compounding eye which Stuart Ziff worked out, but it was too big and wouldn't fit in the face. So we still had design details to work out as we went along.\" Having solved some of the basic geometry problems with the mockup, Allard and his crew turned to building a prototype robot from which all the Number 5s would derive. Since the mechanics of the robot were of the utmost importance, form - out of necessity - followed function. \"I brought in Joss Geiduschek - an engineer I'd worked with at Doug Trumbull's place - and Mike Sorenson and the three of us began working on the mechanical drive system for the main body chassis. Mike had a lot of experience working with experimental aircraft, which is why I wanted him working on the chassis. If you look out the window of a 747 during turbulence, you'll see the engine swinging around out there - it's almost scary. But they are built that way so that they'll be flexible enough to take shock and not fall apart. I felt that flexibility was important for the robot also. It was going to be riding over some rough terrain in the course of shooting and, like an aircraft, it had to be as lightweight as possible. It couldn't be so rigid that it wouldn't be able to take some abuse and handle a variety of situations. We had talked in early meetings about putting little trails down everywhere the robot went. But I knew that once we got into the reality of location shooting, that wouldn't be practical. It had to be flexible enough and durable enough to go anywhere John wanted it to go.\" While working out the drive system of the main body chassis, Allard delegated the electronics work to Richard Chronister and Mark Noel. \"There were some initial electronics problems with the prototype,\" Allard recalled. \"We had some high-tech components, but for the most part I tried to keep it low-tech so that it would be simple enough for any special effects guy to go in and fix. So we were ironing out electronics problems while everything else was going on - still aiming to complete one prototype on which all the problems had been solved. Phil Bartko and Ely Schless were working out the articulations of the head and face. Once we had the mechanics figured out and we knew how everything would attach, Tom Pahk and Chris Ross would go in and design a cover for it. It was always a matter of form following function - we'd make a mechanical form that worked, then Tom and Chris would cover it, matching Syd Mead's drawing as much as possible.\" When a trial run of the prototype proved successful, Allard gave the go-ahead for mass production to begin. \"We made fifteen of everything - fifteen sets of arms, fifteen of all the body parts. The chassis was all aluminum but we used a lot of different materials for various coverings and shells - such as fiberglass, styrene, liquid wood, polyester and epoxy resins. We made lightweight molds from everything because we had a lot of surface detail and that was the only way we could get multiple parts and the only way we could even get them out of the mold. So the bulk of the thing was fiberglass pieces formed out of molds and the rest was high-impact styrene that we vacuformed. We didn't want to have to use grease anywhere on the robot - we knew there would be sand and dirt out on location and if we used grease in the joints it would pick that stuff up. So we used all lubricated polymers for the joints - teflon impregnated delron and things of that nature. One of the things that really saved us, time-wise, was our ability to find things off the shelf that we could use. The hardest thing about this kind of project is getting the parts and materials you need in time. One of the actuators we needed was custom made in Switzerland and they said: 'No problem! We can deliver that to you in four months.' You're always fighting that kind of thing. We had people flying back and forth from Japan on special planes just to get some of the servos we needed fast enough.\" Eight weeks into the project, mass production was completed. Aluminum and fiberglass pieces, mechanical joints and electronic components were stacked everywhere at Allard's facility, waiting to be assembled. \"Just the assembly was a huge job,\" said Allard. \"We had two quys working on the tracks alone for a couple of months. We had used this track that was a conveyor belt for automated production lines in food factories; but since it wasn't designed to be used as tread, the sprockets that drove it caused it to bounce. So we had to modify each sprocket, cut the corner off of each little segment of tread and pop rivet a piece of rubber on to it to give it a tread look. There were thousands and thousands of pieces of track that had to be modified. It was pretty intense because we were up against a shooting deadline. About six weeks prior to the first day of production we gave John and the producers a demonstration of a completed Type 1 robot and they were all smiles. It was the first time they realized that they were going to have something really special.\" One by one, the multiple Number 5s came off the production line. Though their assembly was tedious, the process went smoothly - with one notable exception in which life seemed to imitate art. \"We had robots one through fifteen and, believe it or not, the first one that short-circuited was number five. It was almost spooky. It went up in a puff of smoke and we used Number 5 and his high-tech brethren - equally adept at decimating tanks and mixing martinis - snap to attention before an audience of military and congressional bigwigs on hand to observe a demonstration of their advanced capabilities. The synchronized salute was achieved rather simply by having all five robots' arms controlled by a single radio transmitter. / For Badham and others on the production, Number 5 quickly became an integral member of the cast - and as such, was often spoken to and given direction on the set. / For Number 5, life begins in a shower of sparks as lightning strikes his CINEFEX 28 - 59"],["60","Short Circuit","Inadvertently tossed off a quickly deploys a parachute ground. To accentuate the apparent magnitude of the drop, eight half-scale robots were constructed for the accommodatingly feeds his insatiable thirst for input' by exposing him to books and late-night television. / Startled by Stephanie's dog, Number 5 tumbles off her porch and into a chicken coop. Referring to a video Topazio practices robot arm movements while the film discusses a scene with Ally Sheedy and John Badham scene with the puppet robot. high bridge, Number 5 and drifts safely to the scene. / The foundling Number 5 is taken in by Stephanie Speck (Ally Sheedy) who monitor, puppeteer Rico crew prepares for a twocamera setup. / Head 'roboteer' Tony Urbano prior to photography. / Outside her home in the film, Sheedy rehearses a 60 - CINEFEX 28 that one to solve the electronics difficulties. After we rebuilt it, it ended up being the best, most reliable robot we had for the duration of the show - he seemed to actually exceed his battery life.\" Badham, his crew and actors were already on location in Oregon awaiting the arrival of their star. Though ninety-five percent completed, the Number 5 action props still required minor adjustments when they were loaded on the forty-eightfoot trailer that would take them to location and serve as Allard's portable shop. At the height of production, Allard had employed forty-seven crew members. That crew was now pared down to only fifteen who would accompany him to Oregon to help operate and maintain the robots. Though extremely complex and cleverly constructed, the Number 5 action props were still just inert pieces of equipment. To bring those forms to life took not only the talents of Eric Allard and his crew but also the experience of a group of veteran puppeteers headed by Tony Urbano. \"John Badham was interested in using us,\" Urbano said, \"but we agreed that our hiring would depend on a little screen test he wanted us to do. He gave us six weeks to learn how to work the robot and get used to it, and then he wanted me to present a scene from the picture. I picked the scenes where Number 5 and Ally are out on the deck looking at the sunrise and also the scene with the grasshopper. I felt those two scenes had both humor and dramatic impact. I knew we could handle the funny part - that's what we do all the time - but I wanted to show that we could also carry the deeper moments of the film. So John came in and directed this little test film; and while that was going on, a slender, whitehaired gentleman came in. He was grinning from ear to ear and he kept saying: 'They're great! That's wonderfull' Carl Johnson, one of my puppeteers, looked up at me and said, 'I don't know who that guy is, but I hope he's someone important.' It turned out to be Larry Turman, and the next day we got a call saying we had the job.\" To meet the challenge of creating the main character of a film primarily through puppeteering, Urbano and his crew began rehearsing with the Number 5 puppets as early as possible. \"Even before Eric had them completed,\" Badham related, \"Tony and his guys would go over to the shop and rehearse with them all afternoon. There was a group of four 'roboteers' - which is what I preferred to call them - that regularly operated Number 5, and together with Eric and his people those guys were Number 5. They brought him to life. It was quite a responsibility because Number 5 was the star of the movie. Ally Sheedy and Steve Guttenberg were the costars and I told them that when we first started. I told them if they didn't agree with me, just wait until we'd been shooting for a couple of weeks and see who got all the attention. They took that to heart and were really very good about it.\" In fact, within a few days, the entire cast and crew had become accustomed to the new 'actor' in their midst. \"Ally Sheedy got into it right away,\" Allard recalled. \"She'd come in and say, 'Good morning Number 5, and those"],["61","Short Circuit","CINEFEX 28 • 61"],["62","Short Circuit","of us who were operating him would play along and make him wave or something. Before long, everybody was interacting with this thing as if he were a real character. Even John would say, 'Okay, Number 5, move over here,' just as if he were any other actor. It took about four weeks to get into the flow of things, but eventually we got to a point where the group of us were like one actor, taking direction and moving accordingly.\" Much to their surprise, Tony Urbano and his crew of roboteers - Tim Blaney, Carl Johnson and Rico Topazio - had the opportunity to make their own creative contributions. \"John gave us freedom to create personality and business and movements, all the way down the line. When he hired me, he said he wanted us to give this robot all the acting ability that was necessary to carry the movie. At the time I'd thought: 'Yeah, sure. I'm going to get in there and suggest something and he's going to say no.' But it turned out to be nothing like that. John was so supportive. Most directors we have worked with are not particularly patient with our kind of work - they're not technically oriented and they don't understand when things break down. But John was extremely patient and he encouraged us to take this thing to its limit. It wasn't standard puppeteering because we wanted the character to look real. If we'd done a lot of showy things, he wouldn't have seemed alive - he just would have seemed very cleverly operated. In other words, making him come to 'life' was a matter of not doing too much. We relied on basic animated cartoon theory - starting with a good beginning pose, finding a great ending for the movement and then in-betweening them. We'd decide what his reactions would be to certain things - would he be happy or sad or startled or pensive? And we always tried to build in a moment before he reacted, just a second of time for him to 'think' before he responded. It made him more human and fallible. We tried to get rid of any surplus movement and just leave him with those expressions that really said something and would help the audience relate to him.\" Shots were divided between those that would require the more capable robot puppets and those that needed the remote control models. For both, however, actual operation was a cooperative effort between Eric Allard, Tony Urbano and their respective crews. \"The Type 1 remote-controlled robot took at least four people to operate,\" explained Allard, \"and on some of the more complicated shots as many as nine. I usually drove the base and operated the lower portion; Tim operated the head mechanisms; Tony did the facial expressions and Rico operated the arms. The arms were consistently a problem because the servos in them were just at the limit of their capability - it didn't take much to put them out. Another problem we had with the remote control robots was the fact that there was so much rain on that location. It rained the entire first two weeks we were there and I had told them before we even got there that rain would shut us down. After two weeks of it, everything was wet and we started having some electrical failure. They also started behaving erratically - one robot ran away on us one 62 - CINEFFY 28"],["63","Short Circuit","time and I had to chase it down the street. We were trying to get a shot of the robot going inside the truck and we never did get one we were satisfied with. It just wouldn't behave -it ran away and did what it wanted to do. But considering those horrible wet conditions, they held up remarkably well.\" Electronic disruptions aside, John Badham was more than pleased with his star performer and appreciative of the less-than-ideal conditions in which Allard and company were working. \"It was perfectly understandable,\" Badham conceded. \"Try watching your TV out in the rain sometime and see how well it works. Eric did everything he could - he always had the robot covered with plastic sheeting and little raincoats - but that humidity eventually crept into everything. I began to appreciate why the shark in Jaws caused so much trouble.\" While Eric Allard was responsible for maintaining the remote control robots, continuity of character and 'acting style' demanded that the same roboteers that would be operating the robot puppets for closeup shots have some input in the operation of the long shot robots. \"I was a little nervous about how the remote control robot would perform,\" Urbano confessed. \"Would he cut in all right with the puppet stuff? Number 5 couldn't just be funny and clever and agile in closeups and then when the movie cut to a long shot have him be this stiff, mechanical figure. Because the arms on the remote control ones had limited movement, we had to be careful as to how much movement we could do with the arms of the puppet - again, the two types of robots had to cut together seamlessly in the film.\" Working with radio control mechanisms was rather unusual for puppeteers accustomed to more direct, hands-on techniques. \"Tim Blaney sat at a table about five to ten feet away from the character and operated the hydraulics of the neck and head from there,\" said Urbano. \"The table had a T-bar built onto it and by tipping the T-bar, the head would gimbal. Swiveling the T-bar made the head turn left or right. Also, two switches were built into the T-bar that brought the neck forward or back and raised and lowered the body. So Tim was operating all of that and at the same time saying Number 5's lines. I operated the facial expressions from two radio control panels with eight to ten switches that manipulated his eyes, eyelids and antenna. Usually we had monitors in front of us so we could see what we were doing with the character. Of course in certain situations, like in the catering truck, we didn't have the luxury of monitors and we just had to wing it.\" Each puppeteer became a specialist of sorts in his manipulation of a particular part of Number 5, whether it was the remote-controlled version or the mounted, waist-up puppet. The head and voice were Tim Blaney's specialty; the facial features were always operated by Tony Urbano; and Eric Allard and Rico Topazio controlled the articulation of the cable-actuated arms. Shortly into production, the puppeteers became competent with both types of robots, and which type would be used was dictated by the demands of the sequence. The opening scenes of the film called for an impressive demonstration before a skeptical crowd of military and government officials. The sequence required the services of five of the remote control robots as well as the use of insert arms. At Tony Urbano's suggestion, the 'villain' robots - as all the robots except Number 5 were dubbed - were operated solely by Eric Allard and his crew. \"After spending two months learning to put personality into this thing,\" Urbano commented, \"I was afraid some of that personality would show up in the bad guy robots no matter how hard we tried not to. By having Eric and his group manipulate the five robots at the beginning, we essentially had a different 'actor' behind the machine.\" Though the opening sequence was scripted to be an amazing display of the robots' capabilities - from decimating tanks to mixing a martini - most of the tricks performed by the mechanical show-offs were relatively easy to achieve. \"Some of the things we thought would be the most difficult ended up being the simplest,\" Allard confessed. \"Having them salute exactly together turned out to be a matter of putting the same crystal in all of their arm functions - one radio control did all the saluting, And, by using the insert arms, mixing the martinis wasn't a problem at all. Rico and I worked the sequence out together since we were the two with the most experience manipulating the arms. The only problem with it was that the geometry of the hands didn't allow for a lot of leverage for picking things up. So we helped the hands out by putting double-faced tape on the fingers which gave us a better grip on the glasses. Actually, the most difficult part of the opening sequence turned out to be just getting the robots to run in tight formation down the corridor. It was shot in one big master shot so we had five operators crammed in corners, using video to see what was going on.\" After Number 5's miraculous 'birth' via a freakish act of nature, he distractedly rolls himself aboard a truck which takes him outside the fences of the Nova facility and into the big wide world. Undeterred by a failed attempt to communicate with a gas pump, Number 5 sets out on a consciousness-raising odyssey that is promptly cut short when a close encounter with some freeway traffic sends him tumbling off a bridge. In true James Bond fashion, he deploys a parachute and lands safely on the roof of Stephanie Speck's catering truck. The sequence was shot in three separate phases. The first phase entailed throwing one of the seventy-pound, full-sized stunt robots off a bridge which spanned the Columbia River at the location site in Oregon. \"We used a real Type 1 robot going up the ramp and then we sling-shot a stunt robot off the bridge,\" Allard explained. \"We ended up throwing two of them off the bridge - they fell one hundred and eighty feet and just disintegrated at the bottom. In fact, after shooting this, we were down to only one stunt robot; so we made another four back at the shop in L.A. Fortunately, we already had all the templates, so it wasn't difficult to crank them out.\" Allard had built eight half-scale robots, equipped with little On the set representing Stephanie's living room, John Badham restages one of his classic Saturday Night Fever dance routines with Number 5 standing in for John Travolta. Both the radio-controlled robot and the cable-controlled halfrobot were employed in the sequence, augmented where necessary with marionettestyle wire work. / An airbearing platform - akin to a hovercraft - was provided by Eric Allard. Floating frictionlessly above the floor, the platform supported both puppet and operators and allowed for smooth, flowing dance moves. Two weeks of independent rehearsal were required to choreograph and perfect the difficult routine, which was then shot on stage in only two days. CINEFEX 28 • 63"],["64","Short Circuit","photography - to aid in the provide interaction for the lines, many of which were decided to retain Blaney's distinctive voice, which was re-recorded to enhance audio quality and then turned over to sound effects impresario Frank Serafine for electronic processing. Employing FMmeans of a Y-Expressor - married with Blaney's voice, tracking the dialog precisely. then phase-shifted to give it a more localized quality as if from the robot. As an added subtlety, Number 5's voice youthfulness, while in later reels it was lowered as he Number 5 strikes a proud disassembling the car belonging to Stephanie's obnoxious ex-boyfriend. roboteering effort and actors - puppeteer Tim Blaney read Number 5's ad-libbed. Badham later synthesis techniques, a high-tech metallic digitization sound was created and then - by The combined result was it were actually coming was pitch-shifted in the early reels to impart a suggestion of robotic grew more mature. pose after deftly During principal 64 - CINEFEX 28 parachutes, specifically for the second phase of the sequence in which Number 5 deploys his parachute and floats serenely towards the ground. The miniature scale was not only the most practical way of achieving the shot, it was essential in creating the dramatic and visual effect that Badham was after. \"We used the tallest bridge we could find in the area,\" Badham noted, \"but it still wasn't tall enough to give us a nice long fall. So I reasoned that if we used a three-foot-tall robot instead of a six-foot-tall robot, it would fall twice as long. I also used slowmotion to give it a nice, floating feeling. It was great to watch - the parachutes dutifully opened every time and Mike Sorenson was able to steer them from the ground by remote control. I thought they'd wind up crashing at the bottom, but he was able to guide them so they landed in the river unharmed.\" The final phase of the sequence was shot weeks later on a process stage in Los Angeles. It was just one of several major effects requiring the special talents of special effects supervisor Chuck Gaspar. \"I'd had an idea for getting Number 5 to land on a truck that was actually moving, on location,\" Gaspar recalled. \"I was going to put the catering truck on a forty-foot flatbed, erect scaffolding on each side with a track in between and then travel with the truck itself, dropping Number 5 on the catering truck while it was moving. But John decided it was too complicated and opted for doing it on a process stage instead. Number 5 was hung from an overhead carrier with a sliding door track on piano wire. The catering truck was stationary and we just landed him on it, which was fairly simple. The hard part was getting the parachute to collapse behind him. It kept getting tangled up in piano wire and we had to do about ten takes on it before we got it right.\" To get the full range of expression and gesture as he landed, one of the fully-articulated, remote control robots was used for the scene. Stephanie is blissfully unaware that the mechanical creature has hitched a ride until later that night when sounds of enthusiastic exploration emanate from her truck. Upon investigation, she discovers what she thinks is an extraterrestrial and cautiously lures Number 5 into her home. Once inside, she feeds his insatiable appetite for 'input' by giving him every last bit of reading material she possesses. With a reading ability that would put Evelyn Wood to shame, Number 5 absorbs the texts in amazingly short order. \"That was one of the few times that we actually went for a gag rather than realistic movements,\" Urbano commented. \"I just moved his eyes back and forth as fast as possible while an air hose flipped the pages and Rico did super-quick hand movements.\" Exhausted, Stephanie finally goes to bed, leaving her tireless visitor to continue his questionable education via late-night television. The rather sweet relationship between Stephanie and Number 5 that is first evidenced on the deck outside her home the next morning was actually a step back from what John Badham had intended. \"I included a lot of little scenes and bits of business that established Number 5's attachment to Stephanie,\" Badham related, \"things like her resting her head on his shoulder and hugs and kisses. But when we showed the film in early previews, audiences got real irritated with that - they booed and hollered and hissed, so I wound up taking most of it out. The more romantic and mushy among us were ready to go all the way with it, but the audience was obviously not happy seeing Ally Sheedy kiss and hug a robot.\" The moment is interrupted when Stephanie's mongrel dog barks at the strange intruder who, in startled reaction, tumbles off the edge of the deck and into a chicken coop below. \"We wanted to have his arms flailing backwards as he was going over the edge,\" Allard said, \"and it was one of the few times we had to resort to marionette techniques. Since the arms were always the most limited part of all the robots, there was no way we could have gotten that much movement out of them by remote control. We loosened up all the arm axes and disconnected some of the metal on one of the puppets, put strings on either end and just rotated the arms backwards. Then we cut to one of the stunt robots for the actual fall into the chicken coop. The marionette approach worked real well and we used it in a couple of other instances - in the scene where Number 5 deflects the bullets that Stephanie's boyfriend is shooting at him, for example, and again during the Saturday Night Fever dance when he imitates some of John Travolta's movements.\" Having survived his fall into the chicken coop, Number 5 goes on to explore his surroundings, chasing a grasshopper and joyfully imitating its hop across Stephanie's front lawn. Though the scripted sequence would be an obvious crowd-pleaser, the filmmakers looked forward to the grasshopper chase with particular dread. \"We made a big fuss about it,\" Badham recalled. \"We were just certain that it was going to be a terrible ordeal to get that robot to hop. But as it turned out, it was relatively easy. Chuck Gaspar came up with this outrageous rig and we shot the sequence in the course of just one morning.\" \"For the jumping scenes,\" Gaspar explained, \"Number 5 was suspended on piano wire from an overhead rail that enabled him to move across the area where he was chasing the grasshopper. He was also attached to a cable that we could raise and lower - aided by counterweights - at the same time he was traveling, which gave him the hopping movement. We used one of the stunt robots that had track movement so that every time he came down from a hop, his tracks were still moving. The tracks were designed to tilt up and down so that when we lifted him up, he sort of looked like he was on his tiptoes. It was one of those things that impresses an audience, but wasn't really difficult to do and only took a few hours to shoot. In contrast, later in the film when Number 5 slices the boyfriend's rifle up like a loaf of bread, that was very difficult and probably amounted to a whole month's work for one man. Yet it goes by in a few seconds and no one thinks anything of it.\" Number 5's encounter with the crushed grasshopper brings with it the awful realization that his precious and new-found life is tenuous and that there is no 'reassembling' for vulnerable living beings. Knowing that Nova brass are on their way to take"],["65","Short Circuit","him back to the facility to be disassembled, Number 5 makes a bold escape employing Stephanie's catering truck as his getaway vehicle. \"Just getting him into and out of the truck was very involved,\" noted Gaspar. \"To help him enter, we built a platform with a treadmill on it - just like Number 5's tracks. It was buried in the ground and it had a telescoping ram - a hydraulic cylinder - that would raise up as the robot's tracks met the tracks of the platform. By positioning his arms at the top of the doorway, it would look like he was lifting himself up into the truck.\" Just getting the sometimes errant remotecontrol robot to hit the proper position on the platform was oftentimes a hit-and-miss affair - with the emphasis on the 'miss.' Luckily, getting Number 5 to exit the vehicle was a little easier. \"All we did was make a cable ramp with piano wire and have the robot itself drive down it. A safety line was attached to his back and we slowly let him out under his own power.\" As capable as Number 5 was, he still needed considerable assistance in actually driving the truck. Called a 'blind drive,' the technique is commonplace in Chuck Gaspar's line of work. \"It's the type of effect we do all the time. We take a boatsteering system and drive from another place within the vehicle. It's done hydraulically and you can be hidden anywhere in the vehicle, off-camera. The driver is usually a stuntman and he has vision outside the vehicle. The only problem with it was that it could get very crowded in there - stuntmen, technicians and puppeteers all in a pretty confined space.\" Now a hotly pursued fugitive, Number 5 returns to Stephanie's home for sanctuary. The soft-hearted caterer cannot bring herself to turn him away and the two of them pass the evening dancing. Though the script referred to the dancing in a general way, John Badham knew precisely what dance they would perform. \"It would have been ridiculous for me to use a dance from Flashdance or Grease or from an old Fred Astaire movie. I had filmed one of the classic dances of film history in Saturday Night Fever and that's what it had to be.\" To prepare for the sequence, Number 5 and his roboteers attended dance class for two weeks. \"We looked at what Travolta did and decided what specifically we wanted Number 5 to mimic. In a way, Number 5 reminded me of Travolta during the rehearsal period. John had had to work like hell to do what he did and he had been scared to death about it. Number 5 worked like hell too, rehearsing with Ally for a long time. Eventually, they got really good at it.\" For Eric Allard and Tony Urbano, the dance sequence provided an opportunity to strut their robotic stuff. \"I really wanted to go wild with it,\" Urbano stated. \"There were really only two places in the movie where we could get flamboyant with the puppeteering - the Three Stooges routine and the dance. So we exploited the opportunity to the limit. We tried to incorporate not only the bits of the dance that would be funniest when Number 5 imitated them, but also those parts that would show the widest variety of movement. Eric Allard had a real facility for moving him around the floor and a lot of old marionette CINEFEX 28 • 65"],["66","Short Circuit","66 - CINEFEX 28"],["67","Short Circuit","techniques came in handy. John gave us a whole week to shoot the sequence - this was on a stage in L.A. after we'd returned from location - but it ended up taking only two days. It would have taken even a shorter amount of time except that we had to use very heavy piano wire on him for some of the arm movements and they showed up the first time. So we had to go in and reshoot it with different lighting.\" Eric Allard provided the robotics crew with an air-bearing platform on which to mount the puppet. Working on the same principle as a hovercraft, the pneumatically-operated platform floated two or three thousandths of an inch off the floor and allowed for amazingly smooth, almost frictionless movement. \"We had four operators on the platform with Number 5,\" explained Allard. \"Rico Topazio was at the elbow manipulating the arm movements and Tim Blaney would operate the 1-handle joystick that articulated the head. Then we needed another two operators to guide the platform itself.\" For shots of Number 5 dancing alone, the fully-articulated, Type 1 robot was used, aided considerably by wires attached to his arms. Enjoying the apparent safety of his new home, Number 5 arises the next morning, dons an apron and proceeds to prepare a totally unappetizing breakfast for Stephanie. It was one of only two sequences in which the tool arm that had figured so prominently in the script was actually employed. It had been seen, briefly, earlier in the film when Number 5 uses it to start the ignition of the truck. Now it came in handy as a means of turning a manual egg beater into an electric one. Built by Mark Noel, the tool arm was electronically and pneumatically controlled. Though a remarkable piece of gadgetry, it required the hands-on manipulation of an operator and was therefore used primarily with the puppet. The breakfast-making sequence was one of the more difficult to shoot. \"Anything with digital dexterity like that was very time consuming,\" stated Badham. \"It took us a long time to get Number 5 to make that breakfast and it was a real mess - pancake batter was splattered everywhere. It was hard enough to get Number 5 to do those things in the first place - like cracking an egg or picking up a measuring cup - but to have him do it and look good doing it was even harder.\" The laboriously prepared breakfast would never be consumed. His hideout discovered, Number 5 spends the rest of the movie running for his freakish life with an entire army of Nova security personnel in hot pursuit. Now a seasoned four-day-old, Number 5 no longer relies on Stephanie for protection and, in fact, becomes the aggressor in outsmarting his enemies. Quickly disassembling the car belonging to Stephanie's ex-boyfriend, he taunts the obnoxious suitor by smugly tossing a coinlike fender washer in the air - a trick he picked up on TV from George Raft. The coin-flip would prove to be the most complicated gag of the film for the physical effects crew. \"We suspended the 'coin' between two piano wires,\" Gaspar explained. \"First we grooved the washer and then put the piano wire in the groove and just soldered back over it and blended the wires out by painting them. The piano wire extended out to a square framework about ten feet across. The framework had motorized travelers that would cause the coin to raise and lower plus bearings that rotated the coin as it went up and down. So the robot would flip the coin with his 'thumb' and we'd take it up on the travelers - rotating the whole time - then bring it down, stop the motors and do the whole thing again. It was shot undercranked because to be able to get that coin to come down and land exactly in his hand would have been very difficult at normal speed.\" Finally deciding to fight fire with fire, Nova sends out four 'villain' robots to subdue the errant runaway. Number 5 outsmarts them, however, and - again inspired by his late-night television viewing - rewires their circuitry, turning the lethal, high-tech soldiers into helpless Three Stooges-type buffoons. The time-consuming sequence - the only one in the film requiring complicated manipulations of several robots at the same time - was relegated to second unit. Since the puppet robots were the only ones maneuverable enough to mimic a typical Three Stooges routine, the choreography of the sequence fell to Tony Urbano and his crew. \"It was difficult enough to get a single robot to do what we wanted,\" noted Urbano. \"To do three at once and coordinate all of those movements was really difficult. I had wanted to do that scene in true Three Stooges style - the routines John had given us to study on videotape had a limited number of camera moves and that seemed to be the best way to go for us as well. The camera would just lock in on all three of them and they'd do their thing. So I worked out how it could be done and because I was scheduled to have an entire day to shoot it, I was confident that it could be really spectacular. Unfortunately, we wound up with only four hours in which to do it - and the second unit insisted on shooting it before we really even had time to rehearse. There were twelve of us operating the puppets - four puppeteers and eight technicians - and we did the best we could. In the end, I guess there's nothing to complain about because the sequence always gets a big laugh, but it was John's editing that made it work.\" In the final accounting, Short Circuit's mechanical star came in at $1.4 million - from the first day of building to the last day of filming. \"It was a bargain,\" Badham assessed, \"considering what the star of a movie can cost these days. Eddie Murphy would have cost us twice as much. And, unlike Eddie Murphy, we can use him again.\" For Eric Allard, it was John Badham's mastery of his mechanical star, as much as the effectiveness of Number 5 himself, that brought the film in on budget and with such great success. \"John took everything he had available to him at the moment he needed to get a shot and usedit. Number 5 had thirty-two articulated functions and it was inevitable that every now and then one of them might not be perfect. But John's attitude was that whenever that happened he still had thirty-one other functions at his disposal and he took advantage of what he had to work with. He had a flexible tool and he learned how to use it.\" Number 5's fender washer 'coin-flip' trick presented physical effects coordinator Chuck Gaspar with his most difficult assignment on the film. Attached invisibly between two wires, the washer was suspended from a large steel framework equipped with motorized travelers to make it raise and lower on cue, plus bearings that caused it to rotate at the same time. Eric Allard controlled the robot's coinflipping arm, while Tony Urbano and his crew provided the robot's other articulations. / Ally Sheedy, Number 5 and John Badham prepare for a scene inside Stephanie's catering truck. / Crowded into the rear of the vehicle, Urbano and company provide articulation for the puppet robot hunched over the wheel. CINEFEX 28 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
